{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-85d1141fc1bc8986", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\t\n2\t\n3\tDeferred Audio Pruning with Local Audio–Visual Dynamics for Omni-LLMs\n4\t\n5\t Title:\n6\t\n7\t Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n8\t\n9\t Description:\n10\t\n11\t arXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\n12\t Learn more\n13\t ×\n14\t\n15\t License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n16\t\n17\tarXiv:2608.08794v1 [cs.AI] 09 Aug 2026\n18\t\n19\tDeferred Audio Pruning with Local Audio–Visual Dynamics for Omni-LLMs\n20\t\n21\tKyeongyoon Lee1,\n22\tHongyeob Kim1,\n23\tYoungeun Kim2,\n24\tSungeun Hong1\n25\t\n26\tAbstract\n27\tOmni-modal LLMs jointly process audio, video, and text, but long multimodal sequences incur substantial prefill and KV-cache costs. Existing omni-modal compression methods primarily focus on pre-LLM token reduction, leaving modality-specific compression across the LLM boundary underexplored. We propose A-PACK, a two-stage framework that defers audio pruning until query-conditioned multimodal interactions emerge. Our analysis shows that audio exhibits higher task-relevant information density and representational diversity per token than video. We further find that local audio–visual dynamics provide a more effective cue for visual selection than token-wise matching. We therefore preserve audio and compress video with local dynamics before the LLM, then progressively prune low-relevance audio and visual tokens and their KV-cache entries inside the LLM. Across four benchmarks on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B/3B, A-PACK achieves the strongest average performance among the evaluated prior methods while reducing prefill FLOPs by up to 78% and improving decoding throughput by up to 2.21×\\times.\n28\t\n29\t1 Introduction\n30\t\n31\t(a) Example query with audio and video frames\n32\t\n33\t(b) Early pruning and pointwise matching\n34\t\n35\t(c) Proposed: deferred audio pruning with local dynamics\n36\t\n37\tFigure 1: \n38\tMotivation of our method.\n39\tEarly audio pruning may discard compact but important evidence, and pointwise audio–visual matching may associate temporally distant events.\n40\tWe preserve audio before the LLM and guide visual compression with local audio–visual dynamics. We prune audio later using query-conditioned relevance.\n41\t\n42\tVideo understanding has progressed from general vision–language models (Li et al. 2025a) to architectures that capture richer spatiotemporal information (Cheng et al. 2024; Li et al. 2025b; Chen et al. 2025; Shen et al. 2025b). More recently, omni-modal LLMs have jointly processed audio, video, and text (Xu et al. 2025; Liu et al. 2025; Li et al. 2025c), enabling tasks that require both listening and watching. Their long audio–visual sequences, however, substantially increase prefill computation and KV-cache memory (Shen et al. 2025b).\n43\t\n44\tOmni-modal token compression methods make their modality-selection decisions before the LLM, using cross-modal cues (Tao et al. 2026), audio-conditioned recoverability (Jung et al. 2026), query-adaptive budgets (Yang et al. 2026a), or asymmetric modality selection (Ding et al. 2026). Importantly, this leaves modality-specific compression across the LLM boundary largely unexplored. Before audio, video, and the query are jointly contextualized within the LLM, they remain represented by modality-specific features. Relevance estimates are therefore incomplete. Early pruning may discard evidence that becomes important during multimodal reasoning, especially brief but informative audio events. In addition, token-wise audio–visual matching (Tao et al. 2026; Xing et al. 2026) can overlook temporal locality and connect semantically similar but non-co-occurring events.\n45\t\n46\t(a) Per-token importance\n47\t\n48\t(b) Per-token diversity\n49\t\n50\tFigure 2: Audio tokens show significantly higher per-token importance and diversity than video tokens. Higher occlusion KL indicates greater impact on predictions when removed, while higher effective rank indicates less redundancy.\n51\t\n52\tAlthough existing methods vary in token scoring and modality-budget allocation, modality-specific decisions are largely made before the LLM. Our key insight is that the criterion for token relevance changes across the LLM boundary, from modality-specific features to query-conditioned multimodal context. Before fusion, audio carries dense evidence in few tokens and should be preserved, while the more redundant visual stream can be compressed aggressively using local audio–visual structure, as shown in Figure 1. After fusion, the joint context enables more reliable assessment and pruning of low-relevance tokens across both modalities.\n53\t\n54\tTwo observations support our stage-dependent view. First, audio shows higher task-relevant information density and representational diversity per token than video across AVUT and WorldSense (see Figure 2). Under a fixed budget, retaining audio consistently performs better, while pruning it yields limited savings and risks losing compact, temporally localized evidence. Second, local audio–visual dynamics capture temporal structure missed by token-wise matching. Neighboring audio and visual representations are informative when they co-evolve within short, aligned windows, whereas pointwise similarity may connect recurring but temporally distant content (Tian et al. 2018; Mahmud and Marculescu 2023; Xing et al. 2026).\n55\t\n56\tBased on these observations, we propose Audio-Prioritized Allocation with Context-aware KV-Cache Pruning (A-PACK), a two-stage token compression framework. Before the LLM, we preserve audio and allocate the visual budget using coarse query relevance and local audio–visual dynamics while removing within-video redundancy. Inside the LLM, we progressively prune low-relevance audio and visual tokens together with their corresponding KV-cache entries. The inner-LLM stage thus realizes deferred audio pruning while reducing computation and KV-cache costs.\n57\t\n58\tAcross four omni-modal benchmarks on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B/3B, A-PACK achieves the strongest average performance among the evaluated training-free methods at both operating points. At the more aggressive setting, it reduces prefill FLOPs by up to 78%78\\% while retaining 97.0%97.0\\% of the full-token average performance. It also reduces average GPU memory by 2.32.3 GB on AVUT and delivers 2.21×2.21\\times higher decoding throughput during inference. Main contributions are:\n59\t\n60\t• \n61\t\n62\tWe identify an audio–visual asymmetry in per-token utility, motivating audio preservation before the LLM and deferred pruning until query-conditioned interactions.\n63\t\n64\t• \n65\t\n66\tBy using short-window audio–visual dynamics, we guide visual compression beyond token-wise matching while removing within-video redundancy.\n67\t\n68\t• \n69\t\n70\tWe progressively prune low-relevance audio and visual tokens with their KV-cache entries, reducing prefill, memory, and decoding costs without training.\n71\t\n72\tFigure 3: Overview of our method. Stage 1 (pre-LLM) preserves audio tokens and compresses only video. It allocates more visual tokens to frames with stronger local audio–visual CKA, then removes within-video redundancy through anchor-based token selection. Stage 2 (inner-LLM) progressively prunes query-irrelevant audio and visual tokens after multimodal interaction. Their corresponding KV-cache entries are also removed, realizing deferred audio pruning and reducing decoding cost.\n73\t\n74\t2 Related Work\n75\t\n76\t2.1 Token Compression for Multimodal LLMs\n77\t\n78\tThe high redundancy and computational cost of long visual sequences have motivated token compression for video LLMs.\n79\tVideo-LLM token compression is applied either before or inside the language model. Pre-LLM methods reduce visual tokens through temporal segmentation and density pruning (Shen et al. 2025a) or information-aware allocation (Yuan et al. 2026). Inner-LLM methods prune contextualized tokens using attention (Chen et al. 2024) or text-conditioned relevance (Zhang et al. 2025b). DyCoke further combines early temporal compression with later KV-cache pruning (Tao et al. 2025).\n80\t\n81\tOmni-modal compression additionally requires deciding how audio and video should be treated at different stages. Existing methods use audio saliency (Tao et al. 2026), query-adaptive modality budgets (Yang et al. 2026a), asymmetric selection or cross-modal recoverability (Ding et al. 2026; Jung et al. 2026), or joint audio–visual ranking (Gong et al. 2026). These approaches establish the value of modality-aware compression, but most determine modality reduction before fine-grained query-conditioned interactions form. Prior methods primarily optimize which tokens or modalities to reduce before the LLM. In contrast, A-PACK additionally determines when each modality should be compressed. It preserves audio while reducing visual redundancy before fusion, then switches to joint query-conditioned pruning after fusion.\n82\t\n83\t2.2 Local Audio–Visual Dynamics\n84\t\n85\tAudio–visual correspondence has been studied in self-supervised representation learning and sound-source localization, where acoustic signals are associated with their visible sources (Arandjelović and Zisserman 2018). Event-localization methods further identify short intervals in which audible and visible events co-occur (Tian et al. 2018; Mahmud and Marculescu 2023), while locality-aware approaches model temporal continuity to suppress distant or irrelevant matches (Xing et al. 2026).\n86\t\n87\tRepresentation-level similarity has also been studied with Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA), originally proposed to compare neural representations (Kornblith et al. 2019). It has also been adapted for multimodal analysis and distillation (Yang et al. 2026b). These works primarily use CKA as a training objective or diagnostic measure over global or localized representations. In contrast, A-PACK applies CKA at inference to short, time-aligned audio–visual windows, capturing local representational co-variation rather than individual token similarity. This signal guides visual-budget allocation together with query relevance, while within-video redundancy is handled separately. This formulation provides a locality-aware, training-free signal for visual token allocation in omni-modal compression.\n88\t\n89\t3 Method\n90\t\n91\tWe aim to compress long audio–visual inputs by adapting the compression strategy to both modality and processing stage across the LLM boundary, as illustrated in Figure 3.\n92\t\n93\t3.1 Pre-LLM Local Alignment Compression\n94\t\n95\tThe pre-LLM stage preserves audio as guidance and focuses compression on video. It estimates local audio–visual correspondence, allocates the visual budget across segments and frames, and retains informative tokens while removing within-scene redundancy.\n96\t\n97\tAlignment as a local, structure-level measure.\n98\t\n99\tAudio–visual alignment should capture not only token-wise similarity but also whether the modalities change together over a short interval. We therefore use Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) (Kornblith et al. 2019; Yang et al. 2026b) within a temporal window to compare their covariance structures. CKA is symmetric unlike attention, preserves richer structure than mutual-kkNN, and avoids the Sinkhorn cost and mass constraint of optimal transport, which can force irrelevant matches when visual tokens greatly outnumber audio tokens (Cuturi 2013).\n100\t\n101\tCoarse pass.\n102\t\n103\tWe mean-pool the visual tokens of each frame and resample the audio sequence onto the frame axis, producing paired frame-level embeddings afa_{f} and vfv_{f}. Over a length-ww sliding window 𝒲f={f−⌊w/2⌋,…,f+⌊w/2⌋}\\mathcal{W}_{f}=\\{f{-}\\lfloor w/2\\rfloor,\\dots,f{+}\\lfloor w/2\\rfloor\\} with w=3w{=}3, we stack the mean-centered embeddings into Af,Vf∈ℝw×dA_{f},V_{f}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{w\\times d}. We then compute their linear CKA as the frame-level alignment score:\n104\t\n105\tcf=CKA​(Af,Vf)=∥Vf⊤​Af∥F2∥Af⊤​Af∥F​∥Vf⊤​Vf∥F.c_{f}=\\mathrm{CKA}(A_{f},V_{f})=\\frac{\\lVert V_{f}^{\\top}A_{f}\\rVert_{F}^{2}}{\\lVert A_{f}^{\\top}A_{f}\\rVert_{F}\\,\\lVert V_{f}^{\\top}V_{f}\\rVert_{F}}.\n106\t\n107\t(1)\n108\t\n109\tA larger cfc_{f} indicates that the local audio and visual representations exhibit more similar temporal variation.\n110\t\n111\tGated fine pass.\n112\t\n113\tLet c¯=1F​∑fcf\\bar{c}=\\tfrac{1}{F}\\sum_{f}c_{f} denote the mean window CKA. A short-window statistic may miss temporally offset correspondence, such as narration preceding an action or a sound persisting after the visible event. Consequently, cfc_{f} can be low even when the two streams remain semantically aligned. When the overall alignment is weak, i.e., c¯<τ\\bar{c}<\\tau, we supplement cfc_{f} with the per-frame audio–visual similarity score 1−d^f∈[0,1]1-\\hat{d}_{f}\\in[0,1]. Here, d^f\\hat{d}_{f} is the min–max-normalized direct distance between the visual tokens in frame ff and the audio tokens. This term captures correspondence even when the modalities no longer co-vary within the same window:\n114\t\n115\tsf={cf,c¯≥τ,(1−β)​cf+β​(1−d^f),c¯<τ.s_{f}=\\begin{cases}c_{f},&\\bar{c}\\geq\\tau,\\\\[2.0pt]\n116\t(1-\\beta)\\,c_{f}+\\beta\\,(1-\\hat{d}_{f}),&\\bar{c}<\\tau.\\end{cases}\n117\t\n118\t(2)\n119\t\n120\tThe weight β\\beta balances local co-variation and direct correspondence. Thus, the gated score relies on CKA when local alignment is reliable and uses direct similarity as a fallback for temporally shifted events.\n121\t\n122\tSegmentation and token selection.\n123\t\n124\tWe segment the video using consecutive-frame continuity and allocate the visual budget according to segment importance, following the motivation of CoSeLECT (Devnani et al. 2026). Let 𝒢m\\mathcal{G}_{m} denote the frames in segment mm, rfr_{f} the query relevance of frame ff, and λ\\lambda the balance between peak and sustained relevance. We score each segment as\n125\t\n126\tgm=|𝒢m|​[λ​maxf∈𝒢m⁡rf+(1−λ)​1|𝒢m|​∑f∈𝒢mrf].g_{m}=\\sqrt{|\\mathcal{G}_{m}|}\\left[\\lambda\\max_{f\\in\\mathcal{G}_{m}}r_{f}+(1-\\lambda)\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{G}_{m}|}\\sum_{f\\in\\mathcal{G}_{m}}r_{f}\\right].\n127\t\n128\t(3)\n129\t\n130\tThe maximum term preserves brief query-critical moments, while the mean term rewards evidence sustained throughout the segment. The square-root term accounts for segment duration without allowing long segments to dominate the allocation. Given a total visual budget BvB_{v}, we assign the segment budget as\n131\t\n132\tBm=Bv​gm∑m′gm′,B_{m}=B_{v}\\frac{g_{m}}{\\sum_{m^{\\prime}}g_{m^{\\prime}}},\n133\t\n134\t(4)\n135\t\n136\twhere the denominator normalizes importance across all segments. Within each segment, the frame-level allocation combines query relevance with the local alignment score sfs_{f} from Eq. (2). Both signals reuse cached backbone embeddings, requiring neither recursive search nor an auxiliary encoder (Tang et al. 2025). After allocating the frame budgets, we remove within-scene redundancy through anchor-based token selection. The first frame of each scene serves as the anchor and retains a diverse, high-coverage token set using density-aware farthest-point sampling (DA-FPS) (Climaco and Garcke 2025). DA-FPS favors representative tokens dissimilar to those already selected, yielding compact yet broad visual coverage. For each non-anchor frame, we compare its tokens with anchor tokens at the same spatial positions. We retain the least-similar tokens up to the frame budget, preserving newly appearing evidence while removing content already represented by the anchor.\n137\t\n138\tMethod\n139\tRR (%)\n140\tEfficiency\n141\tBenchmark\n142\tAvg. (%)↑\\uparrow\n143\t\n144\tPrefill FLOPs (T)↓\\downarrow\n145\t\n146\tFinal Ret. (%)↓\\downarrow\n147\t\n148\tAVUT↑\\uparrow\n149\t\n150\tWorldSense↑\\uparrow\n151\t\n152\tVideo-MME↑\\uparrow\n153\t\n154\tDailyOmni↑\\uparrow\n155\t\n156\tQwen2.5-Omni-7B\n157\t\n158\tFull Tokens\n159\t100\n160\t67.9\n161\t100.0\n162\t64.5\n163\t46.8\n164\t66.0\n165\t63.0\n166\t100.0\n167\t\n168\tRandom\n169\t35\n170\t23.0\n171\t41.1\n172\t60.7\n173\t43.2\n174\t65.6\n175\t56.4\n176\t94.0\n177\t\n178\tDyCoke (V&A) (2025)\n179\t\n180\t35\n181\t23.3\n182\t40.9\n183\t59.9\n184\t43.0\n185\t66.0\n186\t56.1\n187\t93.6\n188\t\n189\tFlashVID (2026)\n190\t\n191\t35\n192\t23.8\n193\t27.1\n194\t61.0\n195\t44.6\n196\t-\n197\t57.8\n198\t90.7†\\dagger\n199\t\n200\tFastV (2024)\n201\t\n202\t35\n203\t26.0\n204\t36.5\n205\t60.2\n206\t44.1\n207\t-\n208\t59.4\n209\t90.8†\\dagger\n210\t\n211\tUniComp (2026)\n212\t\n213\t35\n214\t24.2\n215\t42.0\n216\t61.5\n217\t44.3\n218\t65.9\n219\t59.9\n220\t96.3\n221\t\n222\tOmniSelect (2026a)\n223\t\n224\t35\n225\t26.3\n226\t43.5\n227\t62.9\n228\t45.6\n229\t63.6\n230\t60.0\n231\t96.5\n232\t\n233\tOmniZip (2026)\n234\t\n235\t35\n236\t22.9\n237\t40.2\n238\t62.4\n239\t45.3\n240\t66.6\n241\t58.9\n242\t97.0\n243\t\n244\tA-PACK\n245\t35\n246\t22.9\n247\t16.8\n248\t64.0\n249\t46.1\n250\t66.7\n251\t60.8\n252\t98.8\n253\t\n254\tRandom\n255\t25\n256\t16.5\n257\t30.3\n258\t58.0\n259\t41.6\n260\t65.6\n261\t53.3\n262\t90.9\n263\t\n264\tDyCoke (V&A) (2025)\n265\t\n266\t25\n267\t18.5\n268\t33.2\n269\t58.3\n270\t40.3\n271\t66.1\n272\t51.6\n273\t90.0\n274\t\n275\tFlashVID (2026)\n276\t\n277\t25\n278\t17.1\n279\t23.3\n280\t60.2\n281\t43.5\n282\t-\n283\t56.7\n284\t89.0†\\dagger\n285\t\n286\tFastV (2024)\n287\t\n288\t25\n289\t18.5\n290\t21.2\n291\t52.1\n292\t41.0\n293\t64.0\n294\t53.4\n295\t87.6\n296\t\n297\tUniComp (2026)\n298\t\n299\t25\n300\t16.5\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "aac30206-6366-44d4-bfa2-7bd83dde03dc", "uuid": "a54a6082-b93d-4f28-ba85-0b5d27009ef4", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T19:24:43.696Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_text.txt", "content": "\n\nDeferred Audio Pruning with Local Audio–Visual Dynamics for Omni-LLMs\n\n Title:\n\n Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n\n Description:\n\n arXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\n Learn more\n ×\n\n License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license\n\narXiv:2608.08794v1 [cs.AI] 09 Aug 2026\n\nDeferred Audio Pruning with Local Audio–Visual Dynamics for Omni-LLMs\n\nKyeongyoon Lee1,\nHongyeob Kim1,\nYoungeun Kim2,\nSungeun Hong1\n\nAbstract\nOmni-modal LLMs jointly process audio, video, and text, but long multimodal sequences incur substantial prefill and KV-cache costs. Existing omni-modal compression methods primarily focus on pre-LLM token reduction, leaving modality-specific compression across the LLM boundary underexplored. We propose A-PACK, a two-stage framework that defers audio pruning until query-conditioned multimodal interactions emerge. Our analysis shows that audio exhibits higher task-relevant information density and representational diversity per token than video. We further find that local audio–visual dynamics provide a more effective cue for visual selection than token-wise matching. We therefore preserve audio and compress video with local dynamics before the LLM, then progressively prune low-relevance audio and visual tokens and their KV-cache entries inside the LLM. Across four benchmarks on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B/3B, A-PACK achieves the strongest average performance among the evaluated prior methods while reducing prefill FLOPs by up to 78% and improving decoding throughput by up to 2.21×\\times.\n\n1 Introduction\n\n(a) Example query with audio and video frames\n\n(b) Early pruning and pointwise matching\n\n(c) Proposed: deferred audio pruning with local dynamics\n\nFigure 1: \nMotivation of our method.\nEarly audio pruning may discard compact but important evidence, and pointwise audio–visual matching may associate temporally distant events.\nWe preserve audio before the LLM and guide visual compression with local audio–visual dynamics. We prune audio later using query-conditioned relevance.\n\nVideo understanding has progressed from general vision–language models (Li et al. 2025a) to architectures that capture richer spatiotemporal information (Cheng et al. 2024; Li et al. 2025b; Chen et al. 2025; Shen et al. 2025b). More recently, omni-modal LLMs have jointly processed audio, video, and text (Xu et al. 2025; Liu et al. 2025; Li et al. 2025c), enabling tasks that require both listening and watching. Their long audio–visual sequences, however, substantially increase prefill computation and KV-cache memory (Shen et al. 2025b).\n\nOmni-modal token compression methods make their modality-selection decisions before the LLM, using cross-modal cues (Tao et al. 2026), audio-conditioned recoverability (Jung et al. 2026), query-adaptive budgets (Yang et al. 2026a), or asymmetric modality selection (Ding et al. 2026). Importantly, this leaves modality-specific compression across the LLM boundary largely unexplored. Before audio, video, and the query are jointly contextualized within the LLM, they remain represented by modality-specific features. Relevance estimates are therefore incomplete. Early pruning may discard evidence that becomes important during multimodal reasoning, especially brief but informative audio events. In addition, token-wise audio–visual matching (Tao et al. 2026; Xing et al. 2026) can overlook temporal locality and connect semantically similar but non-co-occurring events.\n\n(a) Per-token importance\n\n(b) Per-token diversity\n\nFigure 2: Audio tokens show significantly higher per-token importance and diversity than video tokens. Higher occlusion KL indicates greater impact on predictions when removed, while higher effective rank indicates less redundancy.\n\nAlthough existing methods vary in token scoring and modality-budget allocation, modality-specific decisions are largely made before the LLM. Our key insight is that the criterion for token relevance changes across the LLM boundary, from modality-specific features to query-conditioned multimodal context. Before fusion, audio carries dense evidence in few tokens and should be preserved, while the more redundant visual stream can be compressed aggressively using local audio–visual structure, as shown in Figure 1. After fusion, the joint context enables more reliable assessment and pruning of low-relevance tokens across both modalities.\n\nTwo observations support our stage-dependent view. First, audio shows higher task-relevant information density and representational diversity per token than video across AVUT and WorldSense (see Figure 2). Under a fixed budget, retaining audio consistently performs better, while pruning it yields limited savings and risks losing compact, temporally localized evidence. Second, local audio–visual dynamics capture temporal structure missed by token-wise matching. Neighboring audio and visual representations are informative when they co-evolve within short, aligned windows, whereas pointwise similarity may connect recurring but temporally distant content (Tian et al. 2018; Mahmud and Marculescu 2023; Xing et al. 2026).\n\nBased on these observations, we propose Audio-Prioritized Allocation with Context-aware KV-Cache Pruning (A-PACK), a two-stage token compression framework. Before the LLM, we preserve audio and allocate the visual budget using coarse query relevance and local audio–visual dynamics while removing within-video redundancy. Inside the LLM, we progressively prune low-relevance audio and visual tokens together with their corresponding KV-cache entries. The inner-LLM stage thus realizes deferred audio pruning while reducing computation and KV-cache costs.\n\nAcross four omni-modal benchmarks on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B/3B, A-PACK achieves the strongest average performance among the evaluated training-free methods at both operating points. At the more aggressive setting, it reduces prefill FLOPs by up to 78%78\\% while retaining 97.0%97.0\\% of the full-token average performance. It also reduces average GPU memory by 2.32.3 GB on AVUT and delivers 2.21×2.21\\times higher decoding throughput during inference. Main contributions are:\n\n• \n\nWe identify an audio–visual asymmetry in per-token utility, motivating audio preservation before the LLM and deferred pruning until query-conditioned interactions.\n\n• \n\nBy using short-window audio–visual dynamics, we guide visual compression beyond token-wise matching while removing within-video redundancy.\n\n• \n\nWe progressively prune low-relevance audio and visual tokens with their KV-cache entries, reducing prefill, memory, and decoding costs without training.\n\nFigure 3: Overview of our method. Stage 1 (pre-LLM) preserves audio tokens and compresses only video. It allocates more visual tokens to frames with stronger local audio–visual CKA, then removes within-video redundancy through anchor-based token selection. Stage 2 (inner-LLM) progressively prunes query-irrelevant audio and visual tokens after multimodal interaction. Their corresponding KV-cache entries are also removed, realizing deferred audio pruning and reducing decoding cost.\n\n2 Related Work\n\n2.1 Token Compression for Multimodal LLMs\n\nThe high redundancy and computational cost of long visual sequences have motivated token compression for video LLMs.\nVideo-LLM token compression is applied either before or inside the language model. Pre-LLM methods reduce visual tokens through temporal segmentation and density pruning (Shen et al. 2025a) or information-aware allocation (Yuan et al. 2026). Inner-LLM methods prune contextualized tokens using attention (Chen et al. 2024) or text-conditioned relevance (Zhang et al. 2025b). DyCoke further combines early temporal compression with later KV-cache pruning (Tao et al. 2025).\n\nOmni-modal compression additionally requires deciding how audio and video should be treated at different stages. Existing methods use audio saliency (Tao et al. 2026), query-adaptive modality budgets (Yang et al. 2026a), asymmetric selection or cross-modal recoverability (Ding et al. 2026; Jung et al. 2026), or joint audio–visual ranking (Gong et al. 2026). These approaches establish the value of modality-aware compression, but most determine modality reduction before fine-grained query-conditioned interactions form. Prior methods primarily optimize which tokens or modalities to reduce before the LLM. In contrast, A-PACK additionally determines when each modality should be compressed. It preserves audio while reducing visual redundancy before fusion, then switches to joint query-conditioned pruning after fusion.\n\n2.2 Local Audio–Visual Dynamics\n\nAudio–visual correspondence has been studied in self-supervised representation learning and sound-source localization, where acoustic signals are associated with their visible sources (Arandjelović and Zisserman 2018). Event-localization methods further identify short intervals in which audible and visible events co-occur (Tian et al. 2018; Mahmud and Marculescu 2023), while locality-aware approaches model temporal continuity to suppress distant or irrelevant matches (Xing et al. 2026).\n\nRepresentation-level similarity has also been studied with Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA), originally proposed to compare neural representations (Kornblith et al. 2019). It has also been adapted for multimodal analysis and distillation (Yang et al. 2026b). These works primarily use CKA as a training objective or diagnostic measure over global or localized representations. In contrast, A-PACK applies CKA at inference to short, time-aligned audio–visual windows, capturing local representational co-variation rather than individual token similarity. This signal guides visual-budget allocation together with query relevance, while within-video redundancy is handled separately. This formulation provides a locality-aware, training-free signal for visual token allocation in omni-modal compression.\n\n3 Method\n\nWe aim to compress long audio–visual inputs by adapting the compression strategy to both modality and processing stage across the LLM boundary, as illustrated in Figure 3.\n\n3.1 Pre-LLM Local Alignment Compression\n\nThe pre-LLM stage preserves audio as guidance and focuses compression on video. It estimates local audio–visual correspondence, allocates the visual budget across segments and frames, and retains informative tokens while removing within-scene redundancy.\n\nAlignment as a local, structure-level measure.\n\nAudio–visual alignment should capture not only token-wise similarity but also whether the modalities change together over a short interval. We therefore use Centered Kernel Alignment (CKA) (Kornblith et al. 2019; Yang et al. 2026b) within a temporal window to compare their covariance structures. CKA is symmetric unlike attention, preserves richer structure than mutual-kkNN, and avoids the Sinkhorn cost and mass constraint of optimal transport, which can force irrelevant matches when visual tokens greatly outnumber audio tokens (Cuturi 2013).\n\nCoarse pass.\n\nWe mean-pool the visual tokens of each frame and resample the audio sequence onto the frame axis, producing paired frame-level embeddings afa_{f} and vfv_{f}. Over a length-ww sliding window 𝒲f={f−⌊w/2⌋,…,f+⌊w/2⌋}\\mathcal{W}_{f}=\\{f{-}\\lfloor w/2\\rfloor,\\dots,f{+}\\lfloor w/2\\rfloor\\} with w=3w{=}3, we stack the mean-centered embeddings into Af,Vf∈ℝw×dA_{f},V_{f}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{w\\times d}. We then compute their linear CKA as the frame-level alignment score:\n\ncf=CKA​(Af,Vf)=∥Vf⊤​Af∥F2∥Af⊤​Af∥F​∥Vf⊤​Vf∥F.c_{f}=\\mathrm{CKA}(A_{f},V_{f})=\\frac{\\lVert V_{f}^{\\top}A_{f}\\rVert_{F}^{2}}{\\lVert A_{f}^{\\top}A_{f}\\rVert_{F}\\,\\lVert V_{f}^{\\top}V_{f}\\rVert_{F}}.\n\n(1)\n\nA larger cfc_{f} indicates that the local audio and visual representations exhibit more similar temporal variation.\n\nGated fine pass.\n\nLet c¯=1F​∑fcf\\bar{c}=\\tfrac{1}{F}\\sum_{f}c_{f} denote the mean window CKA. A short-window statistic may miss temporally offset correspondence, such as narration preceding an action or a sound persisting after the visible event. Consequently, cfc_{f} can be low even when the two streams remain semantically aligned. When the overall alignment is weak, i.e., c¯<τ\\bar{c}<\\tau, we supplement cfc_{f} with the per-frame audio–visual similarity score 1−d^f∈[0,1]1-\\hat{d}_{f}\\in[0,1]. Here, d^f\\hat{d}_{f} is the min–max-normalized direct distance between the visual tokens in frame ff and the audio tokens. This term captures correspondence even when the modalities no longer co-vary within the same window:\n\nsf={cf,c¯≥τ,(1−β)​cf+β​(1−d^f),c¯<τ.s_{f}=\\begin{cases}c_{f},&\\bar{c}\\geq\\tau,\\\\[2.0pt]\n(1-\\beta)\\,c_{f}+\\beta\\,(1-\\hat{d}_{f}),&\\bar{c}<\\tau.\\end{cases}\n\n(2)\n\nThe weight β\\beta balances local co-variation and direct correspondence. Thus, the gated score relies on CKA when local alignment is reliable and uses direct similarity as a fallback for temporally shifted events.\n\nSegmentation and token selection.\n\nWe segment the video using consecutive-frame continuity and allocate the visual budget according to segment importance, following the motivation of CoSeLECT (Devnani et al. 2026). Let 𝒢m\\mathcal{G}_{m} denote the frames in segment mm, rfr_{f} the query relevance of frame ff, and λ\\lambda the balance between peak and sustained relevance. We score each segment as\n\ngm=|𝒢m|​[λ​maxf∈𝒢m⁡rf+(1−λ)​1|𝒢m|​∑f∈𝒢mrf].g_{m}=\\sqrt{|\\mathcal{G}_{m}|}\\left[\\lambda\\max_{f\\in\\mathcal{G}_{m}}r_{f}+(1-\\lambda)\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{G}_{m}|}\\sum_{f\\in\\mathcal{G}_{m}}r_{f}\\right].\n\n(3)\n\nThe maximum term preserves brief query-critical moments, while the mean term rewards evidence sustained throughout the segment. The square-root term accounts for segment duration without allowing long segments to dominate the allocation. Given a total visual budget BvB_{v}, we assign the segment budget as\n\nBm=Bv​gm∑m′gm′,B_{m}=B_{v}\\frac{g_{m}}{\\sum_{m^{\\prime}}g_{m^{\\prime}}},\n\n(4)\n\nwhere the denominator normalizes importance across all segments. Within each segment, the frame-level allocation combines query relevance with the local alignment score sfs_{f} from Eq. (2). Both signals reuse cached backbone embeddings, requiring neither recursive search nor an auxiliary encoder (Tang et al. 2025). After allocating the frame budgets, we remove within-scene redundancy through anchor-based token selection. The first frame of each scene serves as the anchor and retains a diverse, high-coverage token set using density-aware farthest-point sampling (DA-FPS) (Climaco and Garcke 2025). DA-FPS favors representative tokens dissimilar to those already selected, yielding compact yet broad visual coverage. For each non-anchor frame, we compare its tokens with anchor tokens at the same spatial positions. We retain the least-similar tokens up to the frame budget, preserving newly appearing evidence while removing content already represented by the anchor.\n\nMethod\nRR (%)\nEfficiency\nBenchmark\nAvg. (%)↑\\uparrow\n\nPrefill FLOPs (T)↓\\downarrow\n\nFinal Ret. (%)↓\\downarrow\n\nAVUT↑\\uparrow\n\nWorldSense↑\\uparrow\n\nVideo-MME↑\\uparrow\n\nDailyOmni↑\\uparrow\n\nQwen2.5-Omni-7B\n\nFull Tokens\n100\n67.9\n100.0\n64.5\n46.8\n66.0\n63.0\n100.0\n\nRandom\n35\n23.0\n41.1\n60.7\n43.2\n65.6\n56.4\n94.0\n\nDyCoke (V&A) (2025)\n\n35\n23.3\n40.9\n59.9\n43.0\n66.0\n56.1\n93.6\n\nFlashVID (2026)\n\n35\n23.8\n27.1\n61.0\n44.6\n-\n57.8\n90.7†\\dagger\n\nFastV (2024)\n\n35\n26.0\n36.5\n60.2\n44.1\n-\n59.4\n90.8†\\dagger\n\nUniComp (2026)\n\n35\n24.2\n42.0\n61.5\n44.3\n65.9\n59.9\n96.3\n\nOmniSelect (2026a)\n\n35\n26.3\n43.5\n62.9\n45.6\n63.6\n60