M module separately to audio and video; Random, OmniZip, and\nOmniSelect also compress both modalities. ‘-’ denotes an out-of-memory (OOM) result. †\\dagger denotes a three-benchmark mean.\n\nMethod\nResource and Quality\nInference Speed\n\nMemory\n(GB)↓\\downarrow\n\nAccuracy\n(%)↑\\uparrow\n\nPrefill\nSpeedup↑\\uparrow\n\nEnd-to-end\nSpeedup↑\\uparrow\n\nFull\n\n24.0\n\n64.5\n\n1.00×\\times\n\n1.00×\\times\n\nFlashVID\n\n22.5\n\n60.2\n\n1.65×\\times\n\n1.73×\\times\n\nDyCoke\n\n22.5\n\n58.3\n\n1.67×\\times\n\n1.77×\\times\n\nFastV\n\n22.6\n\n52.1\n\n1.80×\\times\n\n1.89×\\times\n\nUniComp\n\n22.4\n\n56.9\n\n1.68×\\times\n\n1.77×\\times\n\nOmniZip\n\n21.9\n\n59.3", "numLines": 300, "startLine": 300, "totalLines": 3580}}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1786476300325", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-8cb393f7518edab4", "name": "Read", "input": {"file_path": "/tmp/paper_text.txt", "limit": 300, "offset": 600}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "aac30206-6366-44d4-bfa2-7bd83dde03dc", "uuid": "7b525332-ccd2-4305-ba33-163dea32b26b"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8cb393f7518edab4", "type": "tool_result", "content": "600\t\n601\t1.75×\\times\n602\t\n603\t1.86×\\times\n604\t\n605\tA-PACK\n606\t\n607\t21.7\n608\t\n609\t62.2\n610\t\n611\t1.85×\\times\n612\t\n613\t1.96×\\times\n614\t\n615\tTable 2: Efficiency on AVUT with Qwen2.5-Omni-7B at the 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tier. A-PACK achieves the highest accuracy, lowest memory use, and fastest prefill and end-to-end inference.\n616\t\n617\t3.2 Inner-LLM Query-Aware Compression\n618\t\n619\tPre-LLM representations do not yet contain fine-grained query-conditioned multimodal context. We therefore defer audio pruning until the decoder has integrated audio, video, and the query. Starting from a middle decoder layer, A-PACK progressively reassesses the relevance of retained audio and visual tokens using query attention. At a pruning layer, let 𝒮\\mathcal{S} denote the currently cached multimodal tokens, qq the representation of the last query token, kik_{i} the key of token ii, and PP the pruning ratio. We score each token using scaled query–key attention:\n620\t\n621\tIi=exp⁡(q⊤​ki/d)∑j∈𝒮exp⁡(q⊤​kj/d),i∈𝒮.\\displaystyle I_{i}=\\frac{\\exp\\!\\left(q^{\\top}k_{i}/\\sqrt{d}\\right)}{\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{S}}\\exp\\!\\left(q^{\\top}k_{j}/\\sqrt{d}\\right)},\\qquad i\\in\\mathcal{S}.\n622\t\n623\t(5)\n624\t\n625\tHere, dd is the key dimension. For a multi-head decoder, we average the score across attention heads. A larger IiI_{i} indicates greater relevance to the query, and the score requires no additional forward pass because the query–key interactions are already computed by the decoder. We then retain the highest-scoring (1−P)(1-P) fraction of the current tokens:\n626\t\n627\t𝒮←TopK⌈(1−P)​|𝒮|⌉⁡(𝒮;I).\\displaystyle\\mathcal{S}\\leftarrow\\operatorname{TopK}_{\\lceil(1-P)|\\mathcal{S}|\\rceil}\\left(\\mathcal{S};I\\right).\n628\t\n629\t(6)\n630\t\n631\tPruning begins after early decoder layers establish multimodal context and is repeated at subsequent pruning layers as the representations become more query-conditioned. Starting from N0N_{0} cached tokens, each step retains a (1−P)(1-P) fraction of the current sequence. The remaining tokens and their corresponding key–value entries are removed, reducing attention computation in later layers and throughout autoregressive decoding.\n632\t\n633\t4 Experiments\n634\t\n635\t4.1 Experimental Setup\n636\t\n637\tBenchmarks.\n638\t\n639\tWe evaluate on five audio–visual understanding benchmarks: AVUT (Yang et al. 2025), WorldSense (Hong et al. 2026), DailyOmni (Zhou et al. 2025), Video-MME (Fu et al. 2025), and AVHBench (Sung-Bin et al. 2025). AVUT is audio-centric, while WorldSense covers joint audio–visual understanding across eight domains. DailyOmni focuses on everyday audio–visual events, and Video-MME evaluates general video understanding without subtitles. AVHBench provides balanced binary judgments for audio–visual hallucination.\n640\t\n641\tComparison Methods.\n642\t\n643\tWe compare A-PACK with representative training-free token compressors: FastV (Chen et al. 2024), DyCoke (Tao et al. 2025), OmniZip (Tao et al. 2026), UniComp (Yuan et al. 2026), FlashVID (Fan et al. 2026), OmniSelect (Yang et al. 2026a), and Random.\n644\tAll baselines use the same backbone without fine-tuning. Full Tokens denotes the uncompressed model.\n645\tDyCoke applies its original temporal token merging separately to audio and video. FastV computes attention at layer 33 before pruning visual tokens.\n646\tWe use the released selection/compression cores of UniComp, OmniSelect, and FlashVID, with method-specific settings adjusted for the target RR tiers. OmniZip uses its released setting at the 35%35\\% tier, for the 25%25\\% tier, we set both ratios to 0.750.75 for more aggressive compression.\n647\t\n648\tImplementation Details.\n649\t\n650\tFor fair comparison, we implement all models on the same Qwen2.5-Omni (Xu et al. 2025) 3B/7B backbone and use NVIDIA A6000 48  GB GPUs without fine-tuning.\n651\tFor each backbone, Table 1 reports matched 35%35\\% and 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tiers. RR is the target prefill-FLOPs ratio relative to Full Tokens and Final Ret. is the ratio of tokens remaining at the end of prefill after inner-LLM pruning. A-PACK starts inner-LLM pruning at layers 1414/1818 for 7B/3B using a 10%10\\%/15%15\\% per-layer drop in the 35%35\\%/25%25\\% tiers. Hyperparameter settings are listed in Supplementary Table S2; ablations fix the inner-LLM per-layer drop at P=15%P{=}15\\%, while varying only the parameter under study.\n652\tWe report accuracy and efficiency under the common protocol in Supplementary Section G.\n653\t\n654\t(a) Time-to-first-token (TTFT) breakdown\n655\t\n656\t(b) Decoding throughput\n657\t\n658\tFigure 4: Inference efficiency of Qwen2.5-Omni-7B at the 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tier. (a) TTFT breakdown into the vision tower, preprocessing with pre-LLM compression, and the LLM backbone with inner-LLM compression. (b) Decoding throughput.\n659\t\n660\tFigure 5: A-PACK has the lowest prefilling time on omni-modal benchmarks owing to its inner-LLM stage.\n661\t\n662\t(a) Audio kept ratio (%)\n663\t\n664\t(b) Window-size sensitivity\n665\t\n666\t(c) CKA alignment weight\n667\t\n668\tFigure 6: Key sensitivity analyses.\n669\t(a) Retaining more audio improves accuracy under a fixed total budget.\n670\t(b) WorldSense accuracy peaks with a short temporal window.\n671\t(c) Performance remains stable across the tested alignment weights. Red circle indicates highest score. Baseline indicates per-token matching score accuracy.\n672\t\n673\t4.2 Main Results\n674\t\n675\tWe use LMMs-Eval (Zhang et al. 2025a) for Video-MME and a unified evaluation pipeline for the other benchmarks. Avg. denotes the mean relative accuracy across benchmarks, with Full Tokens normalized to 100%100\\%. Table 1 compares methods at the 35%35\\% and 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tiers on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B and 3B.\n676\tA-PACK achieves the highest average accuracy across both model scales and operating points. On the 7B model, it retains 98.8%98.8\\% of Full-Tokens accuracy at the 35%35\\% tier while reducing prefill FLOPs by about 66%66\\%. At the more aggressive 25%25\\% tier, it retains 97.0%97.0\\% while reducing prefill FLOPs by about 78%78\\% and final token retention to 5.6%5.6\\%. The gains over OmniZip are largest on AVUT and DailyOmni, supporting the use of preserved audio to guide visual compression. The 3B results show the same trend, retaining 97.3%97.3\\% and 95.5%95.5\\% average accuracy at the two tiers, respectively.\n677\t\n678\t4.3 Efficiency Analyses\n679\t\n680\tTable 2 and Supplementary Table S2 report the\n681\tQwen2.5-Omni-7B efficiency results at the 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tier.\n682\tBecause the inner-LLM stage shortens the KV cache, it also accelerates decoding, unlike methods that prune tokens only before the LLM.\n683\t\n684\t(a) Local-window mask\n685\t\n686\t(b) Static cosine\n687\t\n688\t(c) A-PACK\n689\t\n690\t(d) Domain accuracy: static vs. A-PACK\n691\t\n692\tFigure 7: (a) A local-window mask defines locally aligned audio–visual token pairs.\n693\t(b) Static cosine can assign high similarity to temporally distant matches.\n694\t(c) A-PACK uses gated local temporal correspondence to guide visual selection.\n695\t(d) Local-window alignment improves all eight WorldSense domains; the improvement is\n696\tstatistically significant (exact McNemar p=0.0117p{=}0.0117).\n697\t\n698\tFigure 4 breaks down TTFT and reports decoding throughput. A-PACK has slightly higher pre-LLM overhead than OmniZip due to local audio–visual alignment. However, progressive inner-LLM pruning shortens backbone time and the KV cache, reaching 51.451.4 tok/s versus 31.131.1 for OmniZip and 35.135.1 for DyCoke. It also achieves the lowest prefill time across benchmarks (Figure 5). DyCoke has greater pre-LLM overhead and a longer cache, while FastV runs out of memory by attending to the full visual sequence before layer 3. The 3B model shows the same trend (Supplementary Figure S1).\n699\t\n700\t4.4 Ablation Study on Compression Stages\n701\t\n702\tAcross 3B and 7B, A-PACK pre-LLM selection with a random inner stage outperforms random pre-LLM selection with A-PACK inner pruning on both AVUT and DailyOmni (Table 3; Supplementary Table S3). Thus, the pre-LLM stage provides most of the accuracy gain, while query-aware inner pruning recovers the remaining performance and reduces the later sequence cost. Using A-PACK in both stages is best.\n703\t\n704\tMethod\n705\t\n706\tAVUT\n707\t\n708\tDailyOmni\n709\t\n710\tFLOPs↓\\downarrow\n711\t\n712\tPre-LLM\n713\tInner-LLM\n714\t\n715\tAcc. (%)\n716\t\n717\tAcc. (%)\n718\t\n719\tFull\n720\tFull\n721\t\n722\t64.5\n723\t\n724\t63.0\n725\t\n726\t100%\n727\t\n728\tRandom\n729\tRandom\n730\t\n731\t61.5\n732\t\n733\t56.6\n734\t\n735\t38.3%\n736\t\n737\tRandom\n738\tA-PACK\n739\t\n740\t61.6\n741\t\n742\t56.4\n743\t\n744\t32.4%\n745\t\n746\tA-PACK\n747\tRandom\n748\t\n749\t63.3\n750\t\n751\t58.4\n752\t\n753\t38.2%\n754\t\n755\tA-PACK\n756\tFastV\n757\t\n758\t62.6\n759\t\n760\t57.9\n761\t\n762\t32.7%\n763\t\n764\tA-PACK\n765\tDyCoke\n766\t\n767\t63.0\n768\t\n769\t57.8\n770\t\n771\t39.8%\n772\t\n773\tA-PACK\n774\tA-PACK\n775\t\n776\t64.2\n777\t\n778\t58.5\n779\t\n780\t32.4%\n781\t\n782\tTable 3: Stage-combination ablation on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B. All non-base rows use a 50%50\\%\n783\tpre-LLM retention ratio and the same inner budget (per-layer P=15%P{=}15\\%).\n784\t\n785\t4.5 Ablation Study on Pre-LLM Compression\n786\t\n787\tAudio carries denser information per token.\n788\t\n789\tFigure 2 shows that audio carries more task-relevant\n790\tinformation per token than video: masking an audio token changes the answer\n791\tdistribution 9.8×9.8\\times more, and audio representations have 1.7×1.7\\times\n792\thigher effective rank. We therefore preserve audio as guidance and meet the\n793\tpre-LLM budget by compressing video; Supplementary Section D gives the measurement protocol.\n794\t\n795\tPre-LLM retention ratio.\n796\t\n797\tA retention sweep (Supplementary Figure 4(c)) traces the\n798\tpre-LLM accuracy–cost trade-off from 30%30\\% to 50%50\\%.\n799\tAccuracy rises with retention, so for each tier we choose the highest pre-LLM ratio that remains within its prefill-FLOPs budget: 50%50\\% for the 35%35\\% tier and 35%35\\% for the 25%25\\% tier.\n800\t\n801\tType\n802\tMethod\n803\tAVHBench\n804\tWorldSense\n805\t\n806\tAcc. ↑\\uparrow\n807\t\n808\tPrec. ↑\\uparrow\n809\t\n810\tRec. ↑\\uparrow\n811\t\n812\tF1 ↑\\uparrow\n813\t\n814\tYes ↑\\uparrow\n815\t\n816\tAcc. ↑\\uparrow\n817\t\n818\tBase\n819\tBase\n820\t73.6\n821\t72.7\n822\t75.5\n823\t74.1\n824\t51.9\n825\t44.9\n826\t\n827\tRandom\n828\t73.6\n829\t73.0\n830\t74.8\n831\t73.9\n832\t51.3\n833\t44.3\n834\t\n835\tAttention\n836\t73.4\n837\t72.9\n838\t74.5\n839\t73.7\n840\t51.0\n841\t44.6\n842\t\n843\tAudio Sal.\n844\t73.5\n845\t72.9\n846\t74.7\n847\t73.8\n848\t51.2\n849\t44.5\n850\t\n851\tSim.\n852\tAV Cosine\n853\t73.7\n854\t73.3\n855\t74.5\n856\t73.9\n857\t50.9\n858\t44.7\n859\t\n860\tAlign.\n861\tOT\n862\t73.4\n863\t72.9\n864\t74.5\n865\t73.7\n866\t51.1\n867\t44.3\n868\t\n869\tCKA ++ OT\n870\t73.2\n871\t72.7\n872\t74.3\n873\t73.5\n874\t51.1\n875\t44.6\n876\t\n877\tOurs\n878\tGated CKA\n879\t74.0\n880\t72.7\n881\t77.0\n882\t74.8\n883\t53.0\n884\t46.2\n885\t\n886\tSim.: similarity   \n887\tAlign.: alignment\n888\tAudio Sal.: audio saliency   \n889\tOT: optimal transport\n890\t\n891\tTable 4: Alignment-scoring comparison on AVHBench and WorldSense under a fixed compression setting. Acc. denotes accuracy (%); Prec. denotes precision (%); Rec. denotes recall (%); F1 denotes F1 score (%); and Yes denotes the proportion of “Yes” predictions (%).\n892\t\n893\tRetained audio helps audio- and visual-centric tasks.\n894\t\n895\tFixing the total-token budget, we vary the audio kept ratio and\n896\tmeasure accuracy on AVUT and WorldSense. Figure 6(a) shows\n897\tmonotonic gains on both benchmarks: raising audio retention from\n898\t20%20\\% to 80%80\\% improves relative accuracy by about 10%10\\% on AVUT and\n899\t8%8\\% on WorldSense. Thus, even on the comparatively visual WorldSense,\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "aac30206-6366-44d4-bfa2-7bd83dde03dc", "uuid": "8b957171-a8b6-47d7-9b41-5760f5398b8e", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T19:25:02.505Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_text.txt", "content": "\n1.75×\\times\n\n1.86×\\times\n\nA-PACK\n\n21.7\n\n62.2\n\n1.85×\\times\n\n1.96×\\times\n\nTable 2: Efficiency on AVUT with Qwen2.5-Omni-7B at the 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tier. A-PACK achieves the highest accuracy, lowest memory use, and fastest prefill and end-to-end inference.\n\n3.2 Inner-LLM Query-Aware Compression\n\nPre-LLM representations do not yet contain fine-grained query-conditioned multimodal context. We therefore defer audio pruning until the decoder has integrated audio, video, and the query. Starting from a middle decoder layer, A-PACK progressively reassesses the relevance of retained audio and visual tokens using query attention. At a pruning layer, let 𝒮\\mathcal{S} denote the currently cached multimodal tokens, qq the representation of the last query token, kik_{i} the key of token ii, and PP the pruning ratio. We score each token using scaled query–key attention:\n\nIi=exp⁡(q⊤​ki/d)∑j∈𝒮exp⁡(q⊤​kj/d),i∈𝒮.\\displaystyle I_{i}=\\frac{\\exp\\!\\left(q^{\\top}k_{i}/\\sqrt{d}\\right)}{\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal{S}}\\exp\\!\\left(q^{\\top}k_{j}/\\sqrt{d}\\right)},\\qquad i\\in\\mathcal{S}.\n\n(5)\n\nHere, dd is the key dimension. For a multi-head decoder, we average the score across attention heads. A larger IiI_{i} indicates greater relevance to the query, and the score requires no additional forward pass because the query–key interactions are already computed by the decoder. We then retain the highest-scoring (1−P)(1-P) fraction of the current tokens:\n\n𝒮←TopK⌈(1−P)​|𝒮|⌉⁡(𝒮;I).\\displaystyle\\mathcal{S}\\leftarrow\\operatorname{TopK}_{\\lceil(1-P)|\\mathcal{S}|\\rceil}\\left(\\mathcal{S};I\\right).\n\n(6)\n\nPruning begins after early decoder layers establish multimodal context and is repeated at subsequent pruning layers as the representations become more query-conditioned. Starting from N0N_{0} cached tokens, each step retains a (1−P)(1-P) fraction of the current sequence. The remaining tokens and their corresponding key–value entries are removed, reducing attention computation in later layers and throughout autoregressive decoding.\n\n4 Experiments\n\n4.1 Experimental Setup\n\nBenchmarks.\n\nWe evaluate on five audio–visual understanding benchmarks: AVUT (Yang et al. 2025), WorldSense (Hong et al. 2026), DailyOmni (Zhou et al. 2025), Video-MME (Fu et al. 2025), and AVHBench (Sung-Bin et al. 2025). AVUT is audio-centric, while WorldSense covers joint audio–visual understanding across eight domains. DailyOmni focuses on everyday audio–visual events, and Video-MME evaluates general video understanding without subtitles. AVHBench provides balanced binary judgments for audio–visual hallucination.\n\nComparison Methods.\n\nWe compare A-PACK with representative training-free token compressors: FastV (Chen et al. 2024), DyCoke (Tao et al. 2025), OmniZip (Tao et al. 2026), UniComp (Yuan et al. 2026), FlashVID (Fan et al. 2026), OmniSelect (Yang et al. 2026a), and Random.\nAll baselines use the same backbone without fine-tuning. Full Tokens denotes the uncompressed model.\nDyCoke applies its original temporal token merging separately to audio and video. FastV computes attention at layer 33 before pruning visual tokens.\nWe use the released selection/compression cores of UniComp, OmniSelect, and FlashVID, with method-specific settings adjusted for the target RR tiers. OmniZip uses its released setting at the 35%35\\% tier, for the 25%25\\% tier, we set both ratios to 0.750.75 for more aggressive compression.\n\nImplementation Details.\n\nFor fair comparison, we implement all models on the same Qwen2.5-Omni (Xu et al. 2025) 3B/7B backbone and use NVIDIA A6000 48  GB GPUs without fine-tuning.\nFor each backbone, Table 1 reports matched 35%35\\% and 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tiers. RR is the target prefill-FLOPs ratio relative to Full Tokens and Final Ret. is the ratio of tokens remaining at the end of prefill after inner-LLM pruning. A-PACK starts inner-LLM pruning at layers 1414/1818 for 7B/3B using a 10%10\\%/15%15\\% per-layer drop in the 35%35\\%/25%25\\% tiers. Hyperparameter settings are listed in Supplementary Table S2; ablations fix the inner-LLM per-layer drop at P=15%P{=}15\\%, while varying only the parameter under study.\nWe report accuracy and efficiency under the common protocol in Supplementary Section G.\n\n(a) Time-to-first-token (TTFT) breakdown\n\n(b) Decoding throughput\n\nFigure 4: Inference efficiency of Qwen2.5-Omni-7B at the 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tier. (a) TTFT breakdown into the vision tower, preprocessing with pre-LLM compression, and the LLM backbone with inner-LLM compression. (b) Decoding throughput.\n\nFigure 5: A-PACK has the lowest prefilling time on omni-modal benchmarks owing to its inner-LLM stage.\n\n(a) Audio kept ratio (%)\n\n(b) Window-size sensitivity\n\n(c) CKA alignment weight\n\nFigure 6: Key sensitivity analyses.\n(a) Retaining more audio improves accuracy under a fixed total budget.\n(b) WorldSense accuracy peaks with a short temporal window.\n(c) Performance remains stable across the tested alignment weights. Red circle indicates highest score. Baseline indicates per-token matching score accuracy.\n\n4.2 Main Results\n\nWe use LMMs-Eval (Zhang et al. 2025a) for Video-MME and a unified evaluation pipeline for the other benchmarks. Avg. denotes the mean relative accuracy across benchmarks, with Full Tokens normalized to 100%100\\%. Table 1 compares methods at the 35%35\\% and 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tiers on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B and 3B.\nA-PACK achieves the highest average accuracy across both model scales and operating points. On the 7B model, it retains 98.8%98.8\\% of Full-Tokens accuracy at the 35%35\\% tier while reducing prefill FLOPs by about 66%66\\%. At the more aggressive 25%25\\% tier, it retains 97.0%97.0\\% while reducing prefill FLOPs by about 78%78\\% and final token retention to 5.6%5.6\\%. The gains over OmniZip are largest on AVUT and DailyOmni, supporting the use of preserved audio to guide visual compression. The 3B results show the same trend, retaining 97.3%97.3\\% and 95.5%95.5\\% average accuracy at the two tiers, respectively.\n\n4.3 Efficiency Analyses\n\nTable 2 and Supplementary Table S2 report the\nQwen2.5-Omni-7B efficiency results at the 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tier.\nBecause the inner-LLM stage shortens the KV cache, it also accelerates decoding, unlike methods that prune tokens only before the LLM.\n\n(a) Local-window mask\n\n(b) Static cosine\n\n(c) A-PACK\n\n(d) Domain accuracy: static vs. A-PACK\n\nFigure 7: (a) A local-window mask defines locally aligned audio–visual token pairs.\n(b) Static cosine can assign high similarity to temporally distant matches.\n(c) A-PACK uses gated local temporal correspondence to guide visual selection.\n(d) Local-window alignment improves all eight WorldSense domains; the improvement is\nstatistically significant (exact McNemar p=0.0117p{=}0.0117).\n\nFigure 4 breaks down TTFT and reports decoding throughput. A-PACK has slightly higher pre-LLM overhead than OmniZip due to local audio–visual alignment. However, progressive inner-LLM pruning shortens backbone time and the KV cache, reaching 51.451.4 tok/s versus 31.131.1 for OmniZip and 35.135.1 for DyCoke. It also achieves the lowest prefill time across benchmarks (Figure 5). DyCoke has greater pre-LLM overhead and a longer cache, while FastV runs out of memory by attending to the full visual sequence before layer 3. The 3B model shows the same trend (Supplementary Figure S1).\n\n4.4 Ablation Study on Compression Stages\n\nAcross 3B and 7B, A-PACK pre-LLM selection with a random inner stage outperforms random pre-LLM selection with A-PACK inner pruning on both AVUT and DailyOmni (Table 3; Supplementary Table S3). Thus, the pre-LLM stage provides most of the accuracy gain, while query-aware inner pruning recovers the remaining performance and reduces the later sequence cost. Using A-PACK in both stages is best.\n\nMethod\n\nAVUT\n\nDailyOmni\n\nFLOPs↓\\downarrow\n\nPre-LLM\nInner-LLM\n\nAcc. (%)\n\nAcc. (%)\n\nFull\nFull\n\n64.5\n\n63.0\n\n100%\n\nRandom\nRandom\n\n61.5\n\n56.6\n\n38.3%\n\nRandom\nA-PACK\n\n61.6\n\n56.4\n\n32.4%\n\nA-PACK\nRandom\n\n63.3\n\n58.4\n\n38.2%\n\nA-PACK\nFastV\n\n62.6\n\n57.9\n\n32.7%\n\nA-PACK\nDyCoke\n\n63.0\n\n57.8\n\n39.8%\n\nA-PACK\nA-PACK\n\n64.2\n\n58.5\n\n32.4%\n\nTable 3: Stage-combination ablation on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B. All non-base rows use a 50%50\\%\npre-LLM retention ratio and the same inner budget (per-layer P=15%P{=}15\\%).\n\n4.5 Ablation Study on Pre-LLM Compression\n\nAudio carries denser information per token.\n\nFigure 2 shows that audio carries more task-relevant\ninformation per token than video: masking an audio token changes the answer\ndistribution 9.8×9.8\\times more, and audio representations have 1.7×1.7\\times\nhigher effective rank. We therefore preserve audio as guidance and meet the\npre-LLM budget by compressing video; Supplementary Section D gives the measurement protocol.\n\nPre-LLM retention ratio.\n\nA retention sweep (Supplementary Figure 4(c)) traces the\npre-LLM accuracy–cost trade-off from 30%30\\% to 50%50\\%.\nAccuracy rises with retention, so for each tier we choose the highest pre-LLM ratio that remains within its prefill-FLOPs budget: 50%50\\% for the 35%35\\% tier and 35%35\\% for the 25%25\\% tier.\n\nType\nMethod\nAVHBench\nWorldSense\n\nAcc. ↑\\uparrow\n\nPrec. ↑\\uparrow\n\nRec. ↑\\uparrow\n\nF1 ↑\\uparrow\n\nYes ↑\\uparrow\n\nAcc. ↑\\uparrow\n\nBase\nBase\n73.6\n72.7\n75.5\n74.1\n51.9\n44.9\n\nRandom\n73.6\n73.0\n74.8\n73.9\n51.3\n44.3\n\nAttention\n73.4\n72.9\n74.5\n73.7\n51.0\n44.6\n\nAudio Sal.\n73.5\n72.9\n74.7\n73.8\n51.2\n44.5\n\nSim.\nAV Cosine\n73.7\n73.3\n74.5\n73.9\n50.9\n44.7\n\nAlign.\nOT\n73.4\n72.9\n74.5\n73.7\n51.1\n44.3\n\nCKA ++ OT\n73.2\n72.7\n74.3\n73.5\n51.1\n44.6\n\nOurs\nGated CKA\n74.0\n72.7\n77.0\n74.8\n53.0\n46.2\n\nSim.: similarity   \nAlign.: alignment\nAudio Sal.: audio saliency   \nOT: optimal transport\n\nTable 4: Alignment-scoring comparison on AVHBench and WorldSense under a fixed compression setting. Acc. denotes accuracy (%); Prec. denotes precision (%); Rec. denotes recall (%); F1 denotes F1 score (%); and Yes denotes the proportion of “Yes” predictions (%).\n\nRetained audio helps audio- and visual-centric tasks.\n\nFixing the total-token budget, we vary the audio kept ratio and\nmeasure accuracy on AVUT and WorldSense. Figure 6(a) shows\nmonotonic gains on both benchmarks: raising audio retention from\n20%20\\% to 80%80\\% improves relative accuracy by about 10%10\\% on AVUT and\n8%8\\% on WorldSense. Thus, even on the comparatively visual WorldSense,", "numLines": 300, "startLine": 600, "totalLines": 3580}}}
