{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9e4518bbb7fcfa1c", "type": "tool_result", "content": "900\taudio acts as useful guidance rather than competing for the token budget.\n901\tAn LLM judge characterizes AVUT as audio-centric and WorldSense as\n902\tvisual-centric (Supplementary Figures S6 and S7).\n903\t\n904\t4.6 Ablation Study on Hyperparameters\n905\t\n906\tHyperparameter sensitivity.\n907\t\n908\tFigure 6(b) compares spatial CKA at w=1w{=}1, which measures\n909\twithin-frame visual-patch covariance and the co-timed audio interval, with temporal\n910\tCKA over larger windows. The result is highest at w=3w{=}3, suggesting that\n911\ta local history of cross-modal change is useful. Gate-threshold and fine-blend sweeps\n912\tare reported in Supplementary Figures 4(a) and\n913\t4(b); the gate is more sensitive, whereas the fine-blend weight\n914\tis comparatively flat around β=0.90\\beta{=}0.90.\n915\t\n916\tAlignment-score weight.\n917\t\n918\tFigure 6(c) sweeps the CKA weight from 0 (pure visual importance) to 11 (pure CKA) on AVUT. In the 7B model, the score varies by less than 0.20.2 points over the sweep, whereas 3B peaks at a small CKA weight and declines toward pure CKA. Alignment therefore acts as a complementary correction to visual importance rather than a signal that should dominate it.\n919\t\n920\t4.7 Analysis of Audio-Visual Alignment\n921\t\n922\tAlignment-scoring alternatives.\n923\t\n924\tOn AVHBench’s balanced binary judgments, our Gated CKA improves recall and F1 over AV cosine by 2.52.5 and 0.90.9 points, respectively (Table 4), indicating greater robustness to audio–visual hallucinations. It also achieves the best WorldSense accuracy (see Supplementary Section E).\n925\t\n926\tLocal-correspondence validation.\n927\t\n928\tFigure 7 shows why local correspondence is needed. Static cosine can make temporally separated events appear aligned, whereas our method\n929\tretains locally aligned audio–visual dynamics using gated local CKA. On WorldSense, it achieves higher accuracy in all eight domains and significantly more paired corrections than degradations (exact McNemar p=0.0117p{=}0.0117), supporting temporal locality as a useful selection signal.\n930\t\n931\t5 Conclusion\n932\t\n933\tWe frame omni-modal token compression as a stage- and modality-dependent problem. A-PACK preserves information-dense audio while reducing redundant visual content before the LLM, then progressively prunes low-relevance multimodal tokens and their KV-cache entries after query-conditioned interactions emerge. Local audio–visual dynamics further provide a stronger cue for visual selection than token-wise similarity. Across four primary benchmarks, A-PACK reduces prefill FLOPs by up to 78%78\\%, improves decoding throughput by up to 2.21×2.21\\times, and lowers AVUT GPU memory by 2.32.3 GB. These results highlight stage-aware compression across the LLM boundary as a promising principle for efficient omni-modal LLMs.\n934\tAs a training-free approach, our method improves efficiency, though aggressive compression may affect fine-grained or long-range evidence. Its audio-preserving strategy may vary across models and tasks, while local co-variation can be sensitive to temporal offsets. Future work will explore adaptive retention, training-based extensions, and broader omni-modal architectures.\n935\t\n936\tReferences\n937\t\n938\tR. Arandjelović and A. Zisserman (2018)\n939\tObjects that sound.\n940\t\n941\tIn Proceedings of the European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV),\n942\t\n943\tCited by: §2.2.\n944\t\n945\tL. Chen, H. Zhao, T. Liu, S. Bai, J. Lin, C. Zhou, and B. Chang (2024)\n946\tAn image is worth 1/2 tokens after layer 2: plug-and-play inference acceleration for large vision-language models.\n947\t\n948\tIn Proceedings of the European Conference on Computer Vision (ECCV),\n949\t\n950\tCited by: Table S7,\n951\tTable S7,\n952\tTable S7,\n953\tTable S7,\n954\tTable S8,\n955\