{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-87a705bf2f081a53", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1400\t\n1401\t2,136\n1402\t5,302\n1403\tAcc./F1\n1404\t\n1405\tBenchmark summary AVHBench comprises three binary judgment tasks and additionally reports precision, recall, and the proportion of “Yes” predictions.\n1406\t\n1407\tAppendix A Implementation and Evaluation Details\n1408\t\n1409\tA.1 Expanded Benchmark Details\n1410\t\n1411\tWe select benchmarks to test whether compression remains effective across varying audio–visual dependencies,\n1412\ttemporal scales, and reasoning demands. AVUT evaluates audio-centric video understanding through six Audio-Visual Human tasks\n1413\tdesigned to reduce reliance on textual shortcuts (Yang et al. 2025). WorldSense spans 26 tasks across eight real-world domains\n1414\tand tests coupled audio–visual reasoning (Hong et al. 2026). DailyOmni covers six forms of temporal and\n1415\tcross-modal reasoning in everyday clips (Zhou et al. 2025), while Video-MME tests general video\n1416\tunderstanding across short, medium, and long durations without subtitles (Fu et al. 2025). AVHBench is\n1417\tused separately for three balanced binary tasks that diagnose audio–visual hallucination (Sung-Bin et al. 2025).\n1418\t\n1419\tAlgorithm 1 A-PACK Token Compression\n1420\t\n1421\tInput: audio tokens XAX_{A}, visual tokens XVX_{V}, query QQ\n1422\tParameter: ww, BB, τsim\\tau_{\\mathrm{sim}}, τ\\tau, γ\\gamma, β\\beta, LmidL_{\\mathrm{mid}}, PP\n1423\tOutput: compressed token sequence\n1424\t\n1425\t1: Resample audio embeddings onto the visual-frame time axis and mean-pool each frame.\n1426\t\n1427\t2: Segment at smoothed consecutive-frame cosine <τsim<\\tau_{\\mathrm{sim}}.\n1428\t\n1429\t3: Score segment mm from frame–query relevance rfr_{f} and broadcast the score as ufu_{f} to its frames.\n1430\t\n1431\t4: Compute each frame’s local-window CKA cfc_{f} and the base visual score bf=γ​uf+(1−γ)​cfb_{f}=\\gamma u_{f}+(1-\\gamma)c_{f}.\n1432\t\n1433\t5: if clip-mean CKA c¯≤τ\\bar{c}\\leq\\tau then blend cfc_{f} with normalized frame–audio distance d^f\\hat{d}_{f}; else set sf=bfs_{f}=b_{f}.\n1434\t\n1435\t6: Allocate the remaining video budget B−|XA|B-|X_{A}| across frames according to sfs_{f}.\n1436\t\n1437\t7: for each segment do\n1438\t\n1439\t8:  Take the first frame as anchor; the rest as non-anchor.\n1440\t\n1441\t9:  Anchor tokens: density-aware farthest-point sampling (DA-FPS).\n1442\t\n1443\t10:  Non-anchor tokens: keep those least similar to the anchor.\n1444\t\n1445\t11: end for\n1446\t\n1447\t12: Feed the retained audio and video tokens to the LLM.\n1448\t\n1449\t13: for layer ℓ≥Lmid\\ell\\geq L_{\\text{mid}} do\n1450\t\n1451\t14:  Keep the top (1−P)(1-P) fraction by last-query attention; drop the remaining PP and slice their KV-cache entries.\n1452\t\n1453\t15: end for\n1454\t\n1455\t16: return compressed token sequence\n1456\t\n1457\tMethod\n1458\t35%35\\%\n1459\t25%25\\%\n1460\tMethod\n1461\t35%35\\%\n1462\t25%25\\%\n1463\t\n1464\tRandom\n1465\tT\n1466\tT\n1467\tFastV\n1468\t.35.35\n1469\tT\n1470\t\n1471\tDyCoke\n1472\tT\n1473\tT\n1474\tUniComp\n1475\t.41.41\n1476\t.27.27\n1477\t\n1478\tFlashVID\n1479\tT\n1480\tT\n1481\tOmniSelect\n1482\tT\n1483\tT\n1484\t\n1485\tOmniZip\n1486\t.40/.70.40/.70\n1487\t.75/.75.75/.75\n1488\tA-PACK\n1489\t.50/.10.50/.10\n1490\t.35/.15.35/.15\n1491\t\n1492\tBudget parameters 35%35\\%/25%25\\% settings (T: tuned).\n1493\t\n1494\tA.2 Algorithm Overview\n1495\t\n1496\tAlgorithm 1 summarizes pre-LLM allocation (Section 3.1) and query-aware inner-LLM pruning (Section 3.2).\n1497\t\n1498\tStage\n1499\t\n1500\tHyperparameter\n1501\t\n1502\tDefault\n1503\t\n1504\tPre-LLM\n1505\t\n1506\tContinuity threshold τsim\\tau_{\\mathrm{sim}}\n1507\t\n1508\t0.970.97\n1509\t\n1510\tContinuity smoothing σ\\sigma\n1511\t\n1512\t1.01.0\n1513\t\n1514\tSegment weight\n1515\t\n1516\t(max⁡r+mean⁡r)​dur(\\max r+\\operatorname{mean}r)\\sqrt{\\mathrm{dur}}\n1517\t\n1518\tDA-FPS density kk\n1519\t\n1520\t55\n1521\t\n1522\tAnchor priority\n1523\t\n1524\t0.30.3\n1525\t\n1526\tWindow size ww\n1527\t\n1528\t33\n1529\t\n1530\tVisual-score weight γ\\gamma\n1531\t\n1532\t0.90.9\n1533\t\n1534\tDedup coverage threshold\n1535\t\n1536\t0.80.8\n1537\t\n1538\tDA-FPS power\n1539\t\n1540\t0.50.5\n1541\t\n1542\tCKA gate τ\\tau\n1543\t\n1544\t0.780.78\n1545\t\n1546\tFine-blend β\\beta\n1547\t\n1548\t0.900.90\n1549\t\n1550\tPre-LLM retention RpreR_{\\mathrm{pre}} (35%/25%)\n1551\t\n1552\t0.50/0.350.50/0.35\n1553\t\n1554\tInner\n1555\t\n1556\tMid layer LmidL_{\\mathrm{mid}}\n1557\t\n1558\tdepth/2/2 (1414/1818 on 7B/3B)\n1559\t\n1560\tPer-layer pruning PP (35%/25%)\n1561\t\n1562\t10%/15%10\\%/15\\%\n1563\t\n1564\tQuery score\n1565\t\n1566\tlast-query only\n1567\t\n1568\tTable S1: A-PACK hyperparameters used in main evaluations. Paired values correspond to the 35%35\\%/25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tiers; RpreR_{\\mathrm{pre}} and PP denote pre-LLM retention and per-layer pruning, respectively.\n1569\t\n1570\tMethod\n1571\tResource and Quality\n1572\tInference Speed\n1573\t\n1574\tMemory\n1575\t(GB)↓\\downarrow\n1576\t\n1577\tAccuracy\n1578\t(%)↑\\uparrow\n1579\t\n1580\tPrefill\n1581\tSpeedup↑\\uparrow\n1582\t\n1583\tEnd-to-end\n1584\tSpeedup↑\\uparrow\n1585\t\n1586\tFull\n1587\t\n1588\t25.1\n1589\t\n1590\t46.8\n1591\t\n1592\t1.00×\\times\n1593\t\n1594\t1.00×\\times\n1595\t\n1596\tFlashVID\n1597\t\n1598\t25.5\n1599\t\n1600\t43.5\n1601\t\n1602\t1.60×\\times\n1603\t\n1604\t1.59×\\times\n1605\t\n1606\tRandom\n1607\t\n1608\t25.5\n1609\t\n1610\t43.7\n1611\t\n1612\t1.74×\\times\n1613\t\n1614\t1.72×\\times\n1615\t\n1616\tDyCoke\n1617\t\n1618\t25.6\n1619\t\n1620\t40.3\n1621\t\n1622\t1.65×\\times\n1623\t\n1624\t1.65×\\times\n1625\t\n1626\tFastV\n1627\t\n1628\t25.7\n1629\t\n1630\t41.0\n1631\t\n1632\t1.68×\\times\n1633\t\n1634\t1.67×\\times\n1635\t\n1636\tUniComp\n1637\t\n1638\t25.5\n1639\t\n1640\t42.1\n1641\t\n1642\t1.63×\\times\n1643\t\n1644\t1.61×\\times\n1645\t\n1646\tOmniZip\n1647\t\n1648\t22.2\n1649\t\n1650\t42.7\n1651\t\n1652\t1.83×\\times\n1653\t\n1654\t1.83×\\times\n1655\t\n1656\tA-PACK\n1657\t\n1658\t21.9\n1659\t\n1660\t45.3\n1661\t\n1662\t1.94×\\times\n1663\t\n1664\t1.92×\\times\n1665\t\n1666\tTable S2: Efficiency on WorldSense for Qwen2.5-Omni-7B in the 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tier. Best and second-best compressor results are bold and underlined, respectively.\n1667\t\n1668\tHere, BB is the joint audio–video pre-LLM budget, LmidL_{\\mathrm{mid}} is the first decoder pruning layer, and PP is the fraction removed at each such layer. We use P=10%P{=}10\\% and 15%15\\% for the 35%35\\% and 25%25\\% tiers, respectively; Table S2 defines the remaining controls.\n1669\t\n1670\tA.3 Compression Protocol and Hyperparameters\n1671\t\n1672\tAll experiments use the public Qwen2.5-Omni 7B/3B checkpoints without fine-tuning. We define the\n1673\t35%35\\% and 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tiers relative to Full Tokens, rather than by nominal token\n1674\tretention. We match each tier by varying the method-specific parameters summarized below.\n1675\t\n1676\tTuned denotes method-specific values calibrated separately for each backbone and benchmark. FastV applies inner-LLM pruning\n1677\tand retains its released 0.350.35 setting at the 35%35\\% tier, whereas UniComp compresses only visual tokens and therefore uses a\n1678\trelatively higher visual retention to match the FLOPs tiers. For OmniZip and A-PACK, each slash-separated pair gives the audio/\n1679\tvideo settings, respectively. Random, DyCoke, FlashVID, and OmniSelect use their native parameters tuned for each tier; Random uses\n1680\tseed 0. Unless stated otherwise, A-PACK uses Table S2.\n1681\t\n1682\tFigure S1: Prefilling time on Qwen2.5-Omni-3B across three benchmarks.\n1683\tA-PACK is the fastest.\n1684\t\n1685\t(a) TTFT breakdown\n1686\t\n1687\t(b) Decoding throughput\n1688\t\n1689\tFigure S2: Inference speed on Qwen2.5-Omni-3B at the 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tier.\n1690\tA-PACK achieves the shortest time-to-first-token and highest decoding throughput.\n1691\t\n1692\tFigure S3: Accuracy versus final token retention. Left: AVUT and DailyOmni on 7B.\n1693\tRight: WorldSense on 7B and 3B.\n1694\t\n1695\tA.4 Pre-LLM Compression Details\n1696\t\n1697\tThis section details the pre-LLM stage in Section 3.1. The visual-score weight γ\\gamma balances query-relevant segment importance against the local CKA score when ranking frames. We first mean-pool the backbone tokens within each frame. After Gaussian smoothing (width σ\\sigma), a drop in cosine similarity between two consecutive\n1698\tframe embeddings below τsim\\tau_{\\mathrm{sim}} starts a new scene segment. We then distribute the visual-token\n1699\tbudget across the resulting segments: segments more relevant to the query receive more tokens, with a\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "aac30206-6366-44d4-bfa2-7bd83dde03dc", "uuid": "1db048f1-c07f-4e1b-a24f-364d7070ed80", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T19:26:03.240Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_text.txt", "content": "\n2,136\n5,302\nAcc./F1\n\nBenchmark summary AVHBench comprises three binary judgment tasks and additionally reports precision, recall, and the proportion of “Yes” predictions.\n\nAppendix A Implementation and Evaluation Details\n\nA.1 Expanded Benchmark Details\n\nWe select benchmarks to test whether compression remains effective across varying audio–visual dependencies,\ntemporal scales, and reasoning demands. AVUT evaluates audio-centric video understanding through six Audio-Visual Human tasks\ndesigned to reduce reliance on textual shortcuts (Yang et al. 2025). WorldSense spans 26 tasks across eight real-world domains\nand tests coupled audio–visual reasoning (Hong et al. 2026). DailyOmni covers six forms of temporal and\ncross-modal reasoning in everyday clips (Zhou et al. 2025), while Video-MME tests general video\nunderstanding across short, medium, and long durations without subtitles (Fu et al. 2025). AVHBench is\nused separately for three balanced binary tasks that diagnose audio–visual hallucination (Sung-Bin et al. 2025).\n\nAlgorithm 1 A-PACK Token Compression\n\nInput: audio tokens XAX_{A}, visual tokens XVX_{V}, query QQ\nParameter: ww, BB, τsim\\tau_{\\mathrm{sim}}, τ\\tau, γ\\gamma, β\\beta, LmidL_{\\mathrm{mid}}, PP\nOutput: compressed token sequence\n\n1: Resample audio embeddings onto the visual-frame time axis and mean-pool each frame.\n\n2: Segment at smoothed consecutive-frame cosine <τsim<\\tau_{\\mathrm{sim}}.\n\n3: Score segment mm from frame–query relevance rfr_{f} and broadcast the score as ufu_{f} to its frames.\n\n4: Compute each frame’s local-window CKA cfc_{f} and the base visual score bf=γ​uf+(1−γ)​cfb_{f}=\\gamma u_{f}+(1-\\gamma)c_{f}.\n\n5: if clip-mean CKA c¯≤τ\\bar{c}\\leq\\tau then blend cfc_{f} with normalized frame–audio distance d^f\\hat{d}_{f}; else set sf=bfs_{f}=b_{f}.\n\n6: Allocate the remaining video budget B−|XA|B-|X_{A}| across frames according to sfs_{f}.\n\n7: for each segment do\n\n8:  Take the first frame as anchor; the rest as non-anchor.\n\n9:  Anchor tokens: density-aware farthest-point sampling (DA-FPS).\n\n10:  Non-anchor tokens: keep those least similar to the anchor.\n\n11: end for\n\n12: Feed the retained audio and video tokens to the LLM.\n\n13: for layer ℓ≥Lmid\\ell\\geq L_{\\text{mid}} do\n\n14:  Keep the top (1−P)(1-P) fraction by last-query attention; drop the remaining PP and slice their KV-cache entries.\n\n15: end for\n\n16: return compressed token sequence\n\nMethod\n35%35\\%\n25%25\\%\nMethod\n35%35\\%\n25%25\\%\n\nRandom\nT\nT\nFastV\n.35.35\nT\n\nDyCoke\nT\nT\nUniComp\n.41.41\n.27.27\n\nFlashVID\nT\nT\nOmniSelect\nT\nT\n\nOmniZip\n.40/.70.40/.70\n.75/.75.75/.75\nA-PACK\n.50/.10.50/.10\n.35/.15.35/.15\n\nBudget parameters 35%35\\%/25%25\\% settings (T: tuned).\n\nA.2 Algorithm Overview\n\nAlgorithm 1 summarizes pre-LLM allocation (Section 3.1) and query-aware inner-LLM pruning (Section 3.2).\n\nStage\n\nHyperparameter\n\nDefault\n\nPre-LLM\n\nContinuity threshold τsim\\tau_{\\mathrm{sim}}\n\n0.970.97\n\nContinuity smoothing σ\\sigma\n\n1.01.0\n\nSegment weight\n\n(max⁡r+mean⁡r)​dur(\\max r+\\operatorname{mean}r)\\sqrt{\\mathrm{dur}}\n\nDA-FPS density kk\n\n55\n\nAnchor priority\n\n0.30.3\n\nWindow size ww\n\n33\n\nVisual-score weight γ\\gamma\n\n0.90.9\n\nDedup coverage threshold\n\n0.80.8\n\nDA-FPS power\n\n0.50.5\n\nCKA gate τ\\tau\n\n0.780.78\n\nFine-blend β\\beta\n\n0.900.90\n\nPre-LLM retention RpreR_{\\mathrm{pre}} (35%/25%)\n\n0.50/0.350.50/0.35\n\nInner\n\nMid layer LmidL_{\\mathrm{mid}}\n\ndepth/2/2 (1414/1818 on 7B/3B)\n\nPer-layer pruning PP (35%/25%)\n\n10%/15%10\\%/15\\%\n\nQuery score\n\nlast-query only\n\nTable S1: A-PACK hyperparameters used in main evaluations. Paired values correspond to the 35%35\\%/25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tiers; RpreR_{\\mathrm{pre}} and PP denote pre-LLM retention and per-layer pruning, respectively.\n\nMethod\nResource and Quality\nInference Speed\n\nMemory\n(GB)↓\\downarrow\n\nAccuracy\n(%)↑\\uparrow\n\nPrefill\nSpeedup↑\\uparrow\n\nEnd-to-end\nSpeedup↑\\uparrow\n\nFull\n\n25.1\n\n46.8\n\n1.00×\\times\n\n1.00×\\times\n\nFlashVID\n\n25.5\n\n43.5\n\n1.60×\\times\n\n1.59×\\times\n\nRandom\n\n25.5\n\n43.7\n\n1.74×\\times\n\n1.72×\\times\n\nDyCoke\n\n25.6\n\n40.3\n\n1.65×\\times\n\n1.65×\\times\n\nFastV\n\n25.7\n\n41.0\n\n1.68×\\times\n\n1.67×\\times\n\nUniComp\n\n25.5\n\n42.1\n\n1.63×\\times\n\n1.61×\\times\n\nOmniZip\n\n22.2\n\n42.7\n\n1.83×\\times\n\n1.83×\\times\n\nA-PACK\n\n21.9\n\n45.3\n\n1.94×\\times\n\n1.92×\\times\n\nTable S2: Efficiency on WorldSense for Qwen2.5-Omni-7B in the 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tier. Best and second-best compressor results are bold and underlined, respectively.\n\nHere, BB is the joint audio–video pre-LLM budget, LmidL_{\\mathrm{mid}} is the first decoder pruning layer, and PP is the fraction removed at each such layer. We use P=10%P{=}10\\% and 15%15\\% for the 35%35\\% and 25%25\\% tiers, respectively; Table S2 defines the remaining controls.\n\nA.3 Compression Protocol and Hyperparameters\n\nAll experiments use the public Qwen2.5-Omni 7B/3B checkpoints without fine-tuning. We define the\n35%35\\% and 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tiers relative to Full Tokens, rather than by nominal token\nretention. We match each tier by varying the method-specific parameters summarized below.\n\nTuned denotes method-specific values calibrated separately for each backbone and benchmark. FastV applies inner-LLM pruning\nand retains its released 0.350.35 setting at the 35%35\\% tier, whereas UniComp compresses only visual tokens and therefore uses a\nrelatively higher visual retention to match the FLOPs tiers. For OmniZip and A-PACK, each slash-separated pair gives the audio/\nvideo settings, respectively. Random, DyCoke, FlashVID, and OmniSelect use their native parameters tuned for each tier; Random uses\nseed 0. Unless stated otherwise, A-PACK uses Table S2.\n\nFigure S1: Prefilling time on Qwen2.5-Omni-3B across three benchmarks.\nA-PACK is the fastest.\n\n(a) TTFT breakdown\n\n(b) Decoding throughput\n\nFigure S2: Inference speed on Qwen2.5-Omni-3B at the 25%25\\% prefill-FLOPs tier.\nA-PACK achieves the shortest time-to-first-token and highest decoding throughput.\n\nFigure S3: Accuracy versus final token retention. Left: AVUT and DailyOmni on 7B.\nRight: WorldSense on 7B and 3B.\n\nA.4 Pre-LLM Compression Details\n\nThis section details the pre-LLM stage in Section 3.1. The visual-score weight γ\\gamma balances query-relevant segment importance against the local CKA score when ranking frames. We first mean-pool the backbone tokens within each frame. After Gaussian smoothing (width σ\\sigma), a drop in cosine similarity between two consecutive\nframe embeddings below τsim\\tau_{\\mathrm{sim}} starts a new scene segment. We then distribute the visual-token\nbudget across the resulting segments: segments more relevant to the query receive more tokens, with a", "numLines": 300, "startLine": 1400, "totalLines": 3580}}}
