{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b3300c90c133a662", "type": "tool_result", "content": "2000\t\n2001\tTable S5: One-at-a-time parameter sensitivity on a WorldSense subset.\n2002\tEach cell reports the accuracy change Δ\\Delta from the selected setting shown in parentheses.\n2003\t\n2004\tAppendix C Additional Compression Analyses\n2005\t\n2006\tC.1 Stage Contributions\n2007\t\n2008\tThe 33B backbone reproduces the 77B stage-combination finding\n2009\t(Table S3). All non-base rows use a 50%50\\% pre-LLM retention ratio\n2010\tand per-layer P=15%P{=}15\\%. At the lowest prefill cost, using A-PACK in\n2011\tboth stages gives the highest AVUT accuracy and the strongest overall trade-off. Although\n2012\trandom inner pruning is 0.80.8 points higher on DailyOmni, it requires about 24%24\\% more prefill FLOPs\n2013\tand is therefore not comparable at the same cost.\n2014\t\n2015\tAdding the stages one at a time (Table S4) isolates each\n2016\tcontribution. The pre-LLM stage alone already retains 97.7%97.7\\% of full accuracy at\n2017\t44.6%44.6\\% prefill on 7B, and adding the inner-LLM stage reduces prefill from 44.6%44.6\\% to 29.9%29.9\\% while holding\n2018\t97.8%97.8\\%. The 3B backbone behaves the same.\n2019\t\n2020\tC.2 Retention and Parameter Sensitivity\n2021\t\n2022\tTotal token budget.\n2023\t\n2024\tFigure S3 varies the proportion of original\n2025\taudio–visual tokens retained after both stages. The pre-LLM stage first keeps about 50%50\\%, and\n2026\tinner-LLM pruning reduces the sequence to 15%15\\%, 25%25\\%, or 35%35\\%. Accuracy remains stable,\n2027\tchanging by at most 0.40.4 points on AVUT, 0.20.2 on DailyOmni, and 0.20.2 on WorldSense. This stability\n2028\tindicates that inner-LLM compression preserves performance across retention targets, even at 15%15\\%.\n2029\t\n2030\tAdditional sensitivity analyses.\n2031\t\n2032\tFigure S4 first examines the gate threshold, fine-blend\n2033\tweight, and pre-LLM retention ratio. The gate has a large local effect, the\n2034\tfine-blend weight is comparatively stable, and increased pre-LLM retention yields\n2035\tthe expected accuracy–cost trade-off.\n2036\t\n2037\tIndividual parameter sensitivity.\n2038\t\n2039\tTable S5 shows that the\n2040\tcontinuity threshold is the most sensitive segmentation control: changing it moves scene\n2041\tboundaries and consequently changes the budget and selection of every segment. Since its\n2042\tbest value varies across benchmarks, we use τsim=0.97\\tau_{\\mathrm{sim}}{=}0.97 as a robust shared\n2043\tsetting. Lower DA-FPS powers perform better because they temper the density term and retain\n2044\tnovelty-driven coverage, whereas large powers overemphasize dense token neighborhoods.\n2045\tLikewise, k=9k{=}9 and 1111 average density over overly broad neighborhoods, blurring the\n2046\tlocal structure needed for a diverse anchor set. Gate and fine-blend settings are comparatively\n2047\tstable near the selected values. Consistent with Figure 6(b), temporal CKA\n2048\tpeaks at w=3w{=}3; wider windows and the spatial-CKA baseline are worse.\n2049\t\n2050\tAppendix D Audio Utility Analysis\n2051\t\n2052\tD.1 Per-Token Information Density\n2053\t\n2054\tMeasurement.\n2055\t\n2056\tThis is the protocol behind Figure 2. For each\n2057\tmodality m∈{A,V}m\\in\\{A,V\\}, we characterize task-relevant information density as\n2058\t\n2059\tηm=I​(Y;Xm∣Q,S0)Cm,\\eta_{m}=\\frac{I(Y;X_{m}\\mid Q,S_{0})}{C_{m}},\n2060\t\n2061\t(1)\n2062\t\n2063\twhere YY is the answer, XmX_{m} the modality tokens, QQ the query, S0S_{0} the remaining\n2064\tinput, and CmC_{m} the token cost. Over an interval of duration Δ\\Delta, Cm=Rm​ΔC_{m}=R_{m}\\Delta.\n2065\tAudio is represented at about RA≈25R_{A}{\\approx}25 tokens per second, far below the visual rate\n2066\tRVR_{V}; preserving it therefore consumes little of the budget while retaining compact evidence.\n2067\tWe estimate task-relevant information density by mean-replacement occlusion. We store the\n2068\tanswer distribution pbase=softmax​(logits)p_{\\text{base}}=\\mathrm{softmax}(\\text{logits}), replace block kk with\n2069\tits mean embedding, and re-run only the decoder with the sequence length and positional\n2070\tencodings fixed. For fair comparison, a block is one video frame and the audio tokens span the\n2071\tsame time width. We report the per-token normalized divergence as follows.\n2072\t\n2073\tKL/tokenm=1|ℬm|​∑k∈ℬmKL​(pbase∥pocc(k))|blockk|\\text{KL/token}_{m}=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{B}_{m}|}\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal{B}_{m}}\\frac{\\mathrm{KL}\\!\\big(p_{\\text{base}}\\,\\|\\,p_{\\text{occ}}^{(k)}\\big)}{|\\text{block}_{k}|}\n2074\t\n2075\t(2)\n2076\t\n2077\tWe measure representational diversity per token using effective rank (Roy and Vetterli 2007):\n2078\t\n2079\terank​(X)=exp⁡(H​(σ~)),H​(σ~)=−∑iσ~i​ln⁡σ~i,\\mathrm{erank}(X)=\\exp\\!\\big(H(\\tilde{\\sigma})\\big),\\qquad H(\\tilde{\\sigma})=-\\sum_{i}\\tilde{\\sigma}_{i}\\ln\\tilde{\\sigma}_{i},\n2080\t\n2081\t(3)\n2082\t\n2083\twhere HH is the Shannon entropy of the normalized singular-value spectrum σ~\\tilde{\\sigma}.\n2084\tEffective rank counts the independent directions used by a token set, so a lower value indicates\n2085\tgreater redundancy. Both measurements use AVUT and WorldSense.\n2086\t\n2087\tStatistical test.\n2088\t\n2089\tFor each metric, we compare paired audio and video values from\n2090\tthe same AVUT and WorldSense evaluation sample using a one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test with the\n2091\talternative that audio exceeds video. Both task-relevant information density and representational\n2092\tdiversity are significantly higher for audio. The test evaluates the consistency of the audio–video\n2093\tdifference across samples rather than treating individual tokens as independent observations.\n2094\t\n2095\tD.2 Audio Preservation under a Fixed Budget\n2096\t\n2097\tFigure S5 compares OmniZip, which prunes audio, against a not-audio-pruned (NAP) variant that\n2098\tkeeps audio purely as guidance under the same overall budget. NAP disables OmniZip’s audio-token\n2099\tmerge and masking operation, keeps every audio position, and adjusts video merging to preserve the same\n2100\ttotal budget. Removing audio pruning improves accuracy\n2101\ton both benchmarks, with the largest gain on AVUT (+5.1+5.1) and a consistent gain on WorldSense (+1.8+1.8).\n2102\tUnder a fixed budget, discarding audio therefore loses more evidence than the extra visual tokens recover.\n2103\t\n2104\tFigure S5: Audio guidance versus audio pruning at the 25%25\\% budget. Keeping audio as guidance\n2105\timproves accuracy on both AVUT and WorldSense.\n2106\t\n2107\tWhich modality answers, and where audio matters.\n2108\t\n2109\tAn LLM judge classifies the modality\n2110\tindicated by each question and its answer choices as audio, visual, both, or neither\n2111\t(Figure S6). AVUT is predominantly audio-oriented, whereas WorldSense is\n2112\tunexpectedly visual-oriented. DailyOmni is comparatively balanced across audio and visual\n2113\trequirements, making it well suited to omni-modal evaluation, whereas Video-MME is predominantly\n2114\tvisual-centric. Figure S7 then decomposes the audio-keep\n2115\tsweep of Figure 6(a) by these same buckets. On AVUT the audio-centric categories gain +8.9+8.9\n2116\tpoints as the audio-keep ratio grows from 0.20.2 to 0.80.8, while the visual-centric categories stay high\n2117\tand nearly flat. On WorldSense both buckets improve as audio is kept. Audio is thus most valuable\n2118\tprecisely where the question requires it, supporting its use as guidance rather than an early pruning target.\n2119\t\n2120\tFigure S6: Question-modality categories assigned by an LLM judge on WorldSense,\n2121\tAVUT, DailyOmni, and Video-MME.\n2122\t\n2123\tFigure S7: Per-category audio-keep sweep, decomposing Figure 6(a) by the query-modality buckets\n2124\tof Figure S6 (mean category accuracy vs. audio keep ratio; WorldSense 66 audio-/2020\n2125\tvisual-centric categories, AVUT 44/22).\n2126\t\n2127\tAppendix E Audio–Visual Alignment Scoring\n2128\t\n2129\tE.1 Alternative Alignment Scorers\n2130\t\n2131\tAlignment-scoring alternatives.\n2132\t\n2133\tTable 4 compares alignment-scoring functions while keeping the remaining compressor fixed. Base combines the native visual-relevance score with local CKA. Random uses deterministic pseudo-random frame scores that are independent of the audio, video, and question content. Attention scores frames from query–video attention computed with the decoder’s query and key projections. Audio-saliency promotes frames co-timed with audio tokens that differ most from their local audio context. AV cosine compares every visual token with every audio token, assigns each visual token its strongest cross-modal cosine match, and averages the scores within a frame. CKA measures the similarity of audio and visual representation structure in a short time-aligned window. OT assigns frame scores through a global cost-aware matching of all audio and visual tokens. CKA++OT blends normalized CKA and OT scores. Gated CKA uses the visual–CKA score for strongly synchronized clips and otherwise combines local CKA with a token-level audio–visual distance cue.\n2134\t\n2135\tPer-task AVHBench breakdown.\n2136\t\n2137\tTable 4 pools the three AVHBench\n2138\tyes/no judgment tasks. Table S6 separates the same scoring-rule ablation into\n2139\taudio-driven video hallucination (A→\\toV), video-driven audio hallucination (V→\\toA), and\n2140\taudio–visual matching (Match) (Sung-Bin et al. 2025). Gated CKA (A-PACK) most improves the two\n2141\talignment-sensitive tasks (A→\\toV and Match).\n2142\t\n2143\tScoring rule\n2144\t\n2145\tA→\\toV\n2146\t\n2147\tV→\\toA\n2148\t\n2149\tMatch\n2150\t\n2151\tAcc.↑\\uparrow\n2152\t\n2153\tAcc.↑\\uparrow\n2154\t\n2155\tAcc.↑\\uparrow\n2156\t\n2157\tBase frame score\n2158\t\n2159\t72.5\n2160\t\n2161\t76.2\n2162\t\n2163\t71.1\n2164\t\n2165\tRandom score\n2166\t\n2167\t73.7\n2168\t\n2169\t76.2\n2170\t\n2171\t70.3\n2172\t\n2173\tAttention score\n2174\t\n2175\t73.5\n2176\t\n2177\t76.2\n2178\t\n2179\t70.0\n2180\t\n2181\tAudio-saliency score\n2182\t\n2183\t73.9\n2184\t\n2185\t75.9\n2186\t\n2187\t70.3\n2188\t\n2189\tAudio-video cosine\n2190\t\n2191\t74.5\n2192\t\n2193\t76.3\n2194\t\n2195\t69.9\n2196\t\n2197\tOptimal Transport\n2198\t\n2199\t73.5\n2200\t\n2201\t76.0\n2202\t\n2203\t70.0\n2204\t\n2205\tCKA ++ OT\n2206\t\n2207\t73.6\n2208\t\n2209\t75.9\n2210\t\n2211\t69.8\n2212\t\n2213\tGated CKA (A-PACK)\n2214\t\n2215\t74.7\n2216\t\n2217\t75.0\n2218\t\n2219\t72.5\n2220\t\n2221\tTable S6: Per-task AVHBench accuracy for alignment-scoring rules. Best and second-best\n2222\tresults in each column are bold and underlined, respectively.\n2223\t\n2224\tE.2 Paired Prediction Analysis\n2225\t\n2226\tThe matched WorldSense comparison in the main paper changes only the alignment scorer and evaluates\n2227\ton WorldSense. It compares Gated CKA and static cosine on identical questions; an exact two-sided\n2228\tMcNemar test evaluates whether their discordant paired predictions favor one scorer. Together with the\n2229\tdomain-level gains, the significant result supports local temporal correspondence as a useful selection\n2230\tsignal.\n2231\t\n2232\tAppendix F Expanded Benchmark Results\n2233\t\n2234\tThis section expands the consolidated 7B/3B main table (Table 1) with full per-benchmark\n2235\tevidence. Tables S7 and S8 give the per-category AVUT (with Video-MME) and\n2236\tper-domain WorldSense breakdowns for both backbones. These carry the detailed cost columns\n2237\t(prefilling FLOPs, FLOPs ratio, and before-LLM retention) defined in Appendix G.\n2238\tA-PACK stays closest to full-token accuracy on average across categories and domains\n2239\tin both prefill-FLOPs tiers.\n2240\t\n2241\tAppendix G Efficiency and Cost Measurement\n2242\t\n2243\tAccuracy is evaluated with the same pipeline and decoding protocol across all methods and compression settings.\n2244\tOur efficiency protocol follows the analytic, token-count accounting of recent holistic token-merging\n2245\twork (Shao et al. 2025), adapted to an omni-modal (audio + video) model. We report prefilling FLOPs and\n2246\tthe FLOPs ratio, the before-LLM retained-token ratio, time-to-first-token (TTFT) with a module-level\n2247\tbreakdown, decoding throughput, end-to-end latency, and peak GPU memory. The before-LLM\n2248\tretained-token ratio is the proportion of original audio–visual tokens that enter the LLM, and it directly\n2249\tdetermines the prefill sequence length.\n2250\tUnless otherwise noted, we measure CUDA-synchronized end-to-end wall-clock latency on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B\n2251\tusing bfloat16, FlashAttention-2, and greedy decoding on an NVIDIA A6000 GPU at the 35%35\\% and 25%25\\%\n2252\tprefill-FLOPs tiers.\n2253\t\n2254\tG.1 FLOPs Accounting\n2255\t\n2256\tPrefill FLOPs.\n2257\t\n2258\tWe estimate compute analytically from token counts, following the\n2259\tholistic token-merging accounting of Shao et al. (2025) and adapting it to an omni-modal model. One\n2260\ttransformer layer at token length nn costs\n2261\t\n2262\tϕ​(n)=4​n​d2⏟Q,K,V,O proj.+2​n2​d⏟attention+2​n​d​m⏟FFN,\\phi(n)\\;=\\;\\underbrace{4nd^{2}}_{\\text{Q,K,V,O proj.}}\\;+\\;\\underbrace{2n^{2}d}_{\\text{attention}}\\;+\\;\\underbrace{2ndm}_{\\text{FFN}},\n2263\t\n2264\t(4)\n2265\t\n2266\twith hidden size dd and FFN width mm. The three terms are the four attention projections, the\n2267\tquadratic attention, and the feed-forward block. Prefill FLOPs sum this over the actual\n2268\tper-layer token schedule {ni}i=1T\\{n_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{T}. This accounting assigns each method the compute\n2269\tincurred at every layer before and after its pruning events:\n2270\t\n2271\tFpre=∑i=1Tϕ​(ni),Fprefull=T​ϕ​(n0),F_{\\mathrm{pre}}\\;=\\;\\sum_{i=1}^{T}\\phi(n_{i}),\\qquad F_{\\mathrm{pre}}^{\\mathrm{full}}\\;=\\;T\\,\\phi(n_{0}),\n2272\t\n2273\t(5)\n2274\t\n2275\twhere nin_{i} is the length entering layer ii and n0n_{0} is the uncompressed input length. The reported\n2276\tFLOPs Ratio is prefill-only,\n2277\t\n2278\tρFLOPs=FpreFprefull=∑i=1Tϕ​(ni)T​ϕ​(n0),\\rho_{\\mathrm{FLOPs}}\\;=\\;\\frac{F_{\\mathrm{pre}}}{F_{\\mathrm{pre}}^{\\mathrm{full}}}\\;=\\;\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{T}\\phi(n_{i})}{T\\,\\phi(n_{0})},\n2279\t\n2280\t(6)\n2281\t\n2282\tand the Prefilling FLOPs column reports the numerator FpreF_{\\mathrm{pre}} in TFLOPs. The\n2283\tconstants are T=28T{=}28, d=3584d{=}3584, m=18944m{=}18944 for the Qwen2.5-Omni-7B thinker and T=36T{=}36,\n2284\td=2048d{=}2048, m=11008m{=}11008 for 3B. Decoding is fixed at R=100R{=}100 generated tokens and is excluded from\n2285\tthe prefill ratio.\n2286\t\n2287\tPer-layer token schedule.\n2288\t\n2289\tEquation (5) places every method on one basis\n2290\tthrough its schedule {ni}\\{n_{i}\\}. Input-level pruners carry a single post-prune length across all TT\n2291\tlayers, so nin_{i} is constant. A method that prunes at an intermediate layer keeps the full length\n2292\tuntil that layer, so FastV holds ni=n0n_{i}{=}n_{0} up to layer 33 and FlashVID up to layer 2020. A-PACK\n2293\tprunes progressively from LmidL_{\\mathrm{mid}}, so its {ni}\\{n_{i}\\} steps down across the upper layers. Each\n2294\tschedule is read from the recorded per-sample token logs, so ρFLOPs\\rho_{\\mathrm{FLOPs}} reflects measured\n2295\tretention rather than the nominal knob.\n2296\t\n2297\tG.2 Runtime and Memory Measurement\n2298\t\n2299\tPrefill / TTFT and its breakdown.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "aac30206-6366-44d4-bfa2-7bd83dde03dc", "uuid": "8187771c-9827-4d9d-84de-1443336385af", "timestamp": "2026-08-11T19:26:40.958Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_text.txt", "content": "\nTable S5: One-at-a-time parameter sensitivity on a WorldSense subset.\nEach cell reports the accuracy change Δ\\Delta from the selected setting shown in parentheses.\n\nAppendix C Additional Compression Analyses\n\nC.1 Stage Contributions\n\nThe 33B backbone reproduces the 77B stage-combination finding\n(Table S3). All non-base rows use a 50%50\\% pre-LLM retention ratio\nand per-layer P=15%P{=}15\\%. At the lowest prefill cost, using A-PACK in\nboth stages gives the highest AVUT accuracy and the strongest overall trade-off. Although\nrandom inner pruning is 0.80.8 points higher on DailyOmni, it requires about 24%24\\% more prefill FLOPs\nand is therefore not comparable at the same cost.\n\nAdding the stages one at a time (Table S4) isolates each\ncontribution. The pre-LLM stage alone already retains 97.7%97.7\\% of full accuracy at\n44.6%44.6\\% prefill on 7B, and adding the inner-LLM stage reduces prefill from 44.6%44.6\\% to 29.9%29.9\\% while holding\n97.8%97.8\\%. The 3B backbone behaves the same.\n\nC.2 Retention and Parameter Sensitivity\n\nTotal token budget.\n\nFigure S3 varies the proportion of original\naudio–visual tokens retained after both stages. The pre-LLM stage first keeps about 50%50\\%, and\ninner-LLM pruning reduces the sequence to 15%15\\%, 25%25\\%, or 35%35\\%. Accuracy remains stable,\nchanging by at most 0.40.4 points on AVUT, 0.20.2 on DailyOmni, and 0.20.2 on WorldSense. This stability\nindicates that inner-LLM compression preserves performance across retention targets, even at 15%15\\%.\n\nAdditional sensitivity analyses.\n\nFigure S4 first examines the gate threshold, fine-blend\nweight, and pre-LLM retention ratio. The gate has a large local effect, the\nfine-blend weight is comparatively stable, and increased pre-LLM retention yields\nthe expected accuracy–cost trade-off.\n\nIndividual parameter sensitivity.\n\nTable S5 shows that the\ncontinuity threshold is the most sensitive segmentation control: changing it moves scene\nboundaries and consequently changes the budget and selection of every segment. Since its\nbest value varies across benchmarks, we use τsim=0.97\\tau_{\\mathrm{sim}}{=}0.97 as a robust shared\nsetting. Lower DA-FPS powers perform better because they temper the density term and retain\nnovelty-driven coverage, whereas large powers overemphasize dense token neighborhoods.\nLikewise, k=9k{=}9 and 1111 average density over overly broad neighborhoods, blurring the\nlocal structure needed for a diverse anchor set. Gate and fine-blend settings are comparatively\nstable near the selected values. Consistent with Figure 6(b), temporal CKA\npeaks at w=3w{=}3; wider windows and the spatial-CKA baseline are worse.\n\nAppendix D Audio Utility Analysis\n\nD.1 Per-Token Information Density\n\nMeasurement.\n\nThis is the protocol behind Figure 2. For each\nmodality m∈{A,V}m\\in\\{A,V\\}, we characterize task-relevant information density as\n\nηm=I​(Y;Xm∣Q,S0)Cm,\\eta_{m}=\\frac{I(Y;X_{m}\\mid Q,S_{0})}{C_{m}},\n\n(1)\n\nwhere YY is the answer, XmX_{m} the modality tokens, QQ the query, S0S_{0} the remaining\ninput, and CmC_{m} the token cost. Over an interval of duration Δ\\Delta, Cm=Rm​ΔC_{m}=R_{m}\\Delta.\nAudio is represented at about RA≈25R_{A}{\\approx}25 tokens per second, far below the visual rate\nRVR_{V}; preserving it therefore consumes little of the budget while retaining compact evidence.\nWe estimate task-relevant information density by mean-replacement occlusion. We store the\nanswer distribution pbase=softmax​(logits)p_{\\text{base}}=\\mathrm{softmax}(\\text{logits}), replace block kk with\nits mean embedding, and re-run only the decoder with the sequence length and positional\nencodings fixed. For fair comparison, a block is one video frame and the audio tokens span the\nsame time width. We report the per-token normalized divergence as follows.\n\nKL/tokenm=1|ℬm|​∑k∈ℬmKL​(pbase∥pocc(k))|blockk|\\text{KL/token}_{m}=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{B}_{m}|}\\sum_{k\\in\\mathcal{B}_{m}}\\frac{\\mathrm{KL}\\!\\big(p_{\\text{base}}\\,\\|\\,p_{\\text{occ}}^{(k)}\\big)}{|\\text{block}_{k}|}\n\n(2)\n\nWe measure representational diversity per token using effective rank (Roy and Vetterli 2007):\n\nerank​(X)=exp⁡(H​(σ~)),H​(σ~)=−∑iσ~i​ln⁡σ~i,\\mathrm{erank}(X)=\\exp\\!\\big(H(\\tilde{\\sigma})\\big),\\qquad H(\\tilde{\\sigma})=-\\sum_{i}\\tilde{\\sigma}_{i}\\ln\\tilde{\\sigma}_{i},\n\n(3)\n\nwhere HH is the Shannon entropy of the normalized singular-value spectrum σ~\\tilde{\\sigma}.\nEffective rank counts the independent directions used by a token set, so a lower value indicates\ngreater redundancy. Both measurements use AVUT and WorldSense.\n\nStatistical test.\n\nFor each metric, we compare paired audio and video values from\nthe same AVUT and WorldSense evaluation sample using a one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test with the\nalternative that audio exceeds video. Both task-relevant information density and representational\ndiversity are significantly higher for audio. The test evaluates the consistency of the audio–video\ndifference across samples rather than treating individual tokens as independent observations.\n\nD.2 Audio Preservation under a Fixed Budget\n\nFigure S5 compares OmniZip, which prunes audio, against a not-audio-pruned (NAP) variant that\nkeeps audio purely as guidance under the same overall budget. NAP disables OmniZip’s audio-token\nmerge and masking operation, keeps every audio position, and adjusts video merging to preserve the same\ntotal budget. Removing audio pruning improves accuracy\non both benchmarks, with the largest gain on AVUT (+5.1+5.1) and a consistent gain on WorldSense (+1.8+1.8).\nUnder a fixed budget, discarding audio therefore loses more evidence than the extra visual tokens recover.\n\nFigure S5: Audio guidance versus audio pruning at the 25%25\\% budget. Keeping audio as guidance\nimproves accuracy on both AVUT and WorldSense.\n\nWhich modality answers, and where audio matters.\n\nAn LLM judge classifies the modality\nindicated by each question and its answer choices as audio, visual, both, or neither\n(Figure S6). AVUT is predominantly audio-oriented, whereas WorldSense is\nunexpectedly visual-oriented. DailyOmni is comparatively balanced across audio and visual\nrequirements, making it well suited to omni-modal evaluation, whereas Video-MME is predominantly\nvisual-centric. Figure S7 then decomposes the audio-keep\nsweep of Figure 6(a) by these same buckets. On AVUT the audio-centric categories gain +8.9+8.9\npoints as the audio-keep ratio grows from 0.20.2 to 0.80.8, while the visual-centric categories stay high\nand nearly flat. On WorldSense both buckets improve as audio is kept. Audio is thus most valuable\nprecisely where the question requires it, supporting its use as guidance rather than an early pruning target.\n\nFigure S6: Question-modality categories assigned by an LLM judge on WorldSense,\nAVUT, DailyOmni, and Video-MME.\n\nFigure S7: Per-category audio-keep sweep, decomposing Figure 6(a) by the query-modality buckets\nof Figure S6 (mean category accuracy vs. audio keep ratio; WorldSense 66 audio-/2020\nvisual-centric categories, AVUT 44/22).\n\nAppendix E Audio–Visual Alignment Scoring\n\nE.1 Alternative Alignment Scorers\n\nAlignment-scoring alternatives.\n\nTable 4 compares alignment-scoring functions while keeping the remaining compressor fixed. Base combines the native visual-relevance score with local CKA. Random uses deterministic pseudo-random frame scores that are independent of the audio, video, and question content. Attention scores frames from query–video attention computed with the decoder’s query and key projections. Audio-saliency promotes frames co-timed with audio tokens that differ most from their local audio context. AV cosine compares every visual token with every audio token, assigns each visual token its strongest cross-modal cosine match, and averages the scores within a frame. CKA measures the similarity of audio and visual representation structure in a short time-aligned window. OT assigns frame scores through a global cost-aware matching of all audio and visual tokens. CKA++OT blends normalized CKA and OT scores. Gated CKA uses the visual–CKA score for strongly synchronized clips and otherwise combines local CKA with a token-level audio–visual distance cue.\n\nPer-task AVHBench breakdown.\n\nTable 4 pools the three AVHBench\nyes/no judgment tasks. Table S6 separates the same scoring-rule ablation into\naudio-driven video hallucination (A→\\toV), video-driven audio hallucination (V→\\toA), and\naudio–visual matching (Match) (Sung-Bin et al. 2025). Gated CKA (A-PACK) most improves the two\nalignment-sensitive tasks (A→\\toV and Match).\n\nScoring rule\n\nA→\\toV\n\nV→\\toA\n\nMatch\n\nAcc.↑\\uparrow\n\nAcc.↑\\uparrow\n\nAcc.↑\\uparrow\n\nBase frame score\n\n72.5\n\n76.2\n\n71.1\n\nRandom score\n\n73.7\n\n76.2\n\n70.3\n\nAttention score\n\n73.5\n\n76.2\n\n70.0\n\nAudio-saliency score\n\n73.9\n\n75.9\n\n70.3\n\nAudio-video cosine\n\n74.5\n\n76.3\n\n69.9\n\nOptimal Transport\n\n73.5\n\n76.0\n\n70.0\n\nCKA ++ OT\n\n73.6\n\n75.9\n\n69.8\n\nGated CKA (A-PACK)\n\n74.7\n\n75.0\n\n72.5\n\nTable S6: Per-task AVHBench accuracy for alignment-scoring rules. Best and second-best\nresults in each column are bold and underlined, respectively.\n\nE.2 Paired Prediction Analysis\n\nThe matched WorldSense comparison in the main paper changes only the alignment scorer and evaluates\non WorldSense. It compares Gated CKA and static cosine on identical questions; an exact two-sided\nMcNemar test evaluates whether their discordant paired predictions favor one scorer. Together with the\ndomain-level gains, the significant result supports local temporal correspondence as a useful selection\nsignal.\n\nAppendix F Expanded Benchmark Results\n\nThis section expands the consolidated 7B/3B main table (Table 1) with full per-benchmark\nevidence. Tables S7 and S8 give the per-category AVUT (with Video-MME) and\nper-domain WorldSense breakdowns for both backbones. These carry the detailed cost columns\n(prefilling FLOPs, FLOPs ratio, and before-LLM retention) defined in Appendix G.\nA-PACK stays closest to full-token accuracy on average across categories and domains\nin both prefill-FLOPs tiers.\n\nAppendix G Efficiency and Cost Measurement\n\nAccuracy is evaluated with the same pipeline and decoding protocol across all methods and compression settings.\nOur efficiency protocol follows the analytic, token-count accounting of recent holistic token-merging\nwork (Shao et al. 2025), adapted to an omni-modal (audio + video) model. We report prefilling FLOPs and\nthe FLOPs ratio, the before-LLM retained-token ratio, time-to-first-token (TTFT) with a module-level\nbreakdown, decoding throughput, end-to-end latency, and peak GPU memory. The before-LLM\nretained-token ratio is the proportion of original audio–visual tokens that enter the LLM, and it directly\ndetermines the prefill sequence length.\nUnless otherwise noted, we measure CUDA-synchronized end-to-end wall-clock latency on Qwen2.5-Omni-7B\nusing bfloat16, FlashAttention-2, and greedy decoding on an NVIDIA A6000 GPU at the 35%35\\% and 25%25\\%\nprefill-FLOPs tiers.\n\nG.1 FLOPs Accounting\n\nPrefill FLOPs.\n\nWe estimate compute analytically from token counts, following the\nholistic token-merging accounting of Shao et al. (2025) and adapting it to an omni-modal model. One\ntransformer layer at token length nn costs\n\nϕ​(n)=4​n​d2⏟Q,K,V,O proj.+2​n2​d⏟attention+2​n​d​m⏟FFN,\\phi(n)\\;=\\;\\underbrace{4nd^{2}}_{\\text{Q,K,V,O proj.}}\\;+\\;\\underbrace{2n^{2}d}_{\\text{attention}}\\;+\\;\\underbrace{2ndm}_{\\text{FFN}},\n\n(4)\n\nwith hidden size dd and FFN width mm. The three terms are the four attention projections, the\nquadratic attention, and the feed-forward block. Prefill FLOPs sum this over the actual\nper-layer token schedule {ni}i=1T\\{n_{i}\\}_{i=1}^{T}. This accounting assigns each method the compute\nincurred at every layer before and after its pruning events:\n\nFpre=∑i=1Tϕ​(ni),Fprefull=T​ϕ​(n0),F_{\\mathrm{pre}}\\;=\\;\\sum_{i=1}^{T}\\phi(n_{i}),\\qquad F_{\\mathrm{pre}}^{\\mathrm{full}}\\;=\\;T\\,\\phi(n_{0}),\n\n(5)\n\nwhere nin_{i} is the length entering layer ii and n0n_{0} is the uncompressed input length. The reported\nFLOPs Ratio is prefill-only,\n\nρFLOPs=FpreFprefull=∑i=1Tϕ​(ni)T​ϕ​(n0),\\rho_{\\mathrm{FLOPs}}\\;=\\;\\frac{F_{\\mathrm{pre}}}{F_{\\mathrm{pre}}^{\\mathrm{full}}}\\;=\\;\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{T}\\phi(n_{i})}{T\\,\\phi(n_{0})},\n\n(6)\n\nand the Prefilling FLOPs column reports the numerator FpreF_{\\mathrm{pre}} in TFLOPs. The\nconstants are T=28T{=}28, d=3584d{=}3584, m=18944m{=}18944 for the Qwen2.5-Omni-7B thinker and T=36T{=}36,\nd=2048d{=}2048, m=11008m{=}11008 for 3B. Decoding is fixed at R=100R{=}100 generated tokens and is excluded from\nthe prefill ratio.\n\nPer-layer token schedule.\n\nEquation (5) places every method on one basis\nthrough its schedule {ni}\\{n_{i}\\}. Input-level pruners carry a single post-prune length across all TT\nlayers, so nin_{i} is constant. A method that prunes at an intermediate layer keeps the full length\nuntil that layer, so FastV holds ni=n0n_{i}{=}n_{0} up to layer 33 and FlashVID up to layer 2020. A-PACK\nprunes progressively from LmidL_{\\mathrm{mid}}, so its {ni}\\{n_{i}\\} steps down across the upper layers. Each\nschedule is read from the recorded per-sample token logs, so ρFLOPs\\rho_{\\mathrm{FLOPs}} reflects measured\nretention rather than the nominal knob.\n\nG.2 Runtime and Memory Measurement\n\nPrefill / TTFT and its breakdown.", "numLines": 300, "startLine": 2000, "totalLines": 3580}}}
